# Concept: Intelligence (economic definition)
Source: Paper 001, §1, after Legg & Hutter (2007).

Intelligence = 'the ability to achieve goals'. Paper 001's move: this is
usually measured on a single agent in isolation, but the economically
relevant bearer of intelligence is the ORGANIZATION competing in
equilibrium. All other concepts in this section are machinery for saying
how individual intelligence becomes organizational intelligence.
# Concept: Competence
Source: Paper 001, §§1-3.

One of the two capability factors. Competence = domain-specific task
capability (coding, writing, reasoning, robotics...). In the model it is an
aggregator L over the mean of a firm task skills — in the CES case a CES mean
with stretch measure < 1 (the worked example uses the harmonic mean, rho = -1),
so the weakest essential domain bottlenecks output (Kremer O-ring logic).
Effect: competence shifts the LEVEL of output per worker at a given firm
size. It does not change the preferred of the firm size.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 002): competence as MEASURED is not a property
of the model alone. At fixed weights, the harness policy standing between the
model (scroll 1.2.15) changes realized cost/performance by 35-61% on
held-out tasks. Read Paper 001's L as a property of the model+harness
bundle wherever measurement is concerned; the own of the paper model treats
the profile as primitive, which is a modelling choice, not a measurement
fact.
# Concept: Coordination
Source: Paper 001, §§1-3. The second capability factor, and the load-bearing
one in this world-model.

Coordination (y*, per agent; mean z* per firm) = the capabilities that let
agents work better together: shared norms, low communication cost, low
agency friction. Effect: coordination governs the SCALE factor S(m, z*)/m —
how average product changes as firm size m grows. Below the threshold
(z* < 1 in the normalized CES form) average product eventually falls with
size, giving a finite preferred firm size. Coordination, not competence,
sets how big an organization can in a useful way get.

Delta-link 2026-11-06 (Paper 002): Paper 001's coordination runs BETWEEN
agents. Paper 002 exhibits a second axis — coordination ACROSS TIME
within one agent: append-only history coordinates the agent with its past
selves, and the guardian coordinates its one who puts a change forward-self with its
activator-authority (scrolls 1.2.19-1.2.20). The world-model now treats
'coordination' as covering both axes; whether the across-time axis moves
the between-agents gamma is open question Q6 (scroll 3.2.2).
# Concept: Capability profile, capability envelope, mosaic intelligence
Source: Paper 001, §§2.1-2.2, 3.1.

Capability profile: the vector of an agent y = (y1, y2, ..., y*) — task skills
plus a coordination coordinate. Human profiles are jagged: strong in one
domain, weak in another, differing person to person.

Capability envelope E(A): the closed convex hull of the profiles of a set
of agents A — every mean profile a team drawn from A can assemble.

Mosaic intelligence: because the straight of the envelope boundary cuts across
the curved competence contours, mixing complementary specialists reaches
interior mean profiles with competence far above the standalone of any member
competence. In the worked example, coder (9,1) + writer (1,9) teams reach
L = 5 vs 1.8 solo — a near-3x gain with no individual improving at all.
Copies of one AI model share the SAME jaggedness, so replication alone
creates no mosaic; that asymmetry is structural, not a skill gap.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 004): the mosaic also exists INSIDE one
trained network, learned from inside itself. In Paper 004's mixture of
experts (scroll 1.2.29), expert networks develop jagged, complementary
profiles on their own — in the block-level mixture, one expert takes
large parallel factors (81% of its weight), another takes loop tiling
and running in a pipeline — and a gating network assembles the mixture per input.
Unlike Paper 001's firms, where profiles are starting gifts sorted by a
market, here the profiles and the sorting are both TRAINED, together.
The mosaic is also fragile in a way Paper 001's is not: gating tends to
collapse onto the single best expert (polarization, scroll 1.2.31),
melting away the envelope back to one profile unless actively prevented.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 003): first EMPIRICAL sighting of an envelope,
in output space. Paper 003 embeds 61,608 stories (one human + five LLMs
per prompt) in a 304-feature narrative space (scroll 1.2.25): the five
AI sources form one tight cluster — mean AI-AI centroid distance 4.3 vs
human-AI 6.6, and even the CLOSEST human-AI pair is farther apart than
the most distant AI-AI pair — while human stories are 22% more dispersed
around their own centroid. Read through this scroll: different AI
MODELS, not just copies of one, share narrative jaggedness — the AI
envelope in this domain is narrow and shared, the human envelope broad.
Replication and even cross-lab diversity, so far, do not manufacture
mosaic diversity in narrative choice. Per-model outer reaches are nonetheless
distinguishable (fingerprint features, scroll 1.2.26) — profiles differ
within the cluster.
# Concept: Being able to be copied again vs fixed supply (scarcity rents)
Source: Paper 001, §§1, 2.2.

Human expertise is in fixed supply: scarce talent commands Ricardian
scarcity rents (wages far above the outside option — 6x for lone coders,
~10x for coordinator profiles in the worked example). AI expertise is reproducible
at compute cost r per copy: copies can be replicated without limit, which
eliminates scarcity rents on the AI side and caps what an AI capability can
charge at its replication cost. This is difference #1 of the two structural
AI/human differences. Difference #2 is coordination (see canonical scroll
1.1.1/1.1.1/1.2.3 and the supercooperation scroll 1.2.7).
# Concept: Preferred size and the Coasean ceiling
Source: Paper 001, §§2.2, 5.1 (Proposition 1).

Preferred size m*(z*) = the input that gives the largest value over m of average product S(m, z*)/m for the mean of a firm coordination z*. Competence does not enter it: at fixed
composition, better task skills raise output but leave preferred size
unchanged. With human coordination < 1, preferred size is finite — firms
that outgrow it split. This finite preferred size IS the Coasean ceiling in
the model. Proposition 1 bounds almost every active size of the firm between
outer reaches M and M-bar that depend on AI coordination and the human supply
constraint (a firm also cannot be larger than the talent stock allows).
# Concept: Supercooperation threshold and the concentration phase transition
Source: Paper 001, §§2.3, 5.2 (Proposition 3).

Scale returns are governed by gamma, the coordination-driven returns
parameter (gamma = y* in the worked CES cases). Three patterns:
- gamma < 1 (below the line): average product of a large firm vanishes; as the
  market grows (N -> the smallest value) the industry expands by REPLICATING bounded-size
  firms and the market share of any one firm -> 0.
- gamma > 1 (above the line in working together): one large AI firm produces Q at cost of
  order Q^(1/gamma), with 1/gamma < 1 — splitting output across firms loses
  the scale advantage. Equilibrium piles up: a relaxed HHI -> 1, almost
  all output in firm types as large as the market.
- gamma = 1 is the excluded knife-edge.
Crossing the threshold is a PHASE TRANSITION in market structure, driven
by a coordination improvement that may be tiny and benchmark-invisible.
The paper flags the styling: regulation, competing AI providers,
capacity constraints and rigid contracts would likely prevent a literal
one-firm singularity in practice.
# Concept: The size amplifier
Source: Paper 001, §5.2 (Proposition 2).

When AI coordination y*_M rises (still < 1), firm size responds through two
channels: a direct effect (higher mean coordination raises preferred size
at fixed composition, always >= 0) and a composition effect (the firm
re-mixes toward/away from machines, changing mean coordination again).
- If AI coordinates BETTER than humans (y*_H < y*_M < 1): both effects are
  positive — composition AMPLIFIES the direct gain (illustrated machine
  share 0.54 -> 0.83 as firms grow).
- If AI coordinates WORSE (y*_M < y*_H): the composition effect is
  ambiguous and can Soften growth (illustrated machine share 0.38 -> 0.46
  with size gains partly offset).
So the sign of the coordination gap decides whether AI progress compounds
or self-brakes inside the firm.
# Concept: Firm composition patterns — mixed vs separate, human vs AI-only
Source: Paper 001, §5.3 (Proposition 4).

The cost-adjusted best average-product envelope a(lambda, w) — best output
per unit cost at human share lambda — decides who works with whom:
- Machine-only: if AI dominates EVERY point of the human capability hull
  (y_M > all of co(Y_H)) and compute is no costing more than wages (r <= w), all
  firms are machine-only.
- Humans in the loop: if the envelope rises above its machine-only endpoint
  (mosaic intelligence pays) and w <= r, some firms must employ humans.
- Curvature then decides the form: envelope strictly CONVEX in lambda ->
  separate pure-human and pure-AI firms; strictly CONCAVE -> mixed firms,
  all sharing the unique optimal human share.
Same curvature variable returns in displacement dynamics (scroll 1.2.10).
# Concept: Step by step vs sudden displacement
Source: Paper 001, §6.2 (Proposition 5).

Human displacement has two dynamically different forms, selected by the
curvature of the composition envelope a(lambda):
- CONCAVE (mixed-firm regime): displacement is WITHIN-firm. As AI improves,
  firms continuously lower their human share; employment, output and firm
  size move continuously. Full unemployment only when adding a first human
  to an all-machine industry does not pay even at the reservation wage.
- CONVEX (separate-firm regime): displacement is ACROSS-firm. Human-only
  and AI-only firms coexist until a cost inequality flips, then human firms
  are wiped out in jumps — full employment to full unemployment at
  a threshold, wages max{w, r*a(1)/a(0)}.
As AI coordination improves the envelope can itself flip concave -> convex,
so an economy can see step by step erosion first, then a sudden spike.
Task-model form (Proposition 7): for one human type, humans are absent
from every equilibrium exactly when
  max_d (y_d^H / y_d^M) + (y*_H - y*_M) * log(1 + m*(y*_M)) <= w / r
— best relative task productivity PLUS the human coordination advantage
valued at machine-only firm size. A coordination advantage can therefore
cover a task-productivity gap; under constant returns the second
term vanishes and only the task race remains.
# Concept: Wages as bids — shadow value and sorting
Source: Paper 001, §6.1 (Theorem 2), §4.2.

In equilibrium the wage of a worker equals the highest BID for them: firms bid
the marginal of the worker contribution to firm surplus (a bid function of their
full capability profile, evaluated at the size of the firm, mix and scale).
Consequences: (a) pay can rise without the worker changing at all, when
complementary teammates or scale raise their marginal contribution
(coders' pay grew to near three times the size when writers arrived); (b) standalone-weak
coordinator profiles can be among the highest paid, because value of the coordination
scales with the whole team; (c) workers SORT — each type is won by the firm
type whose bid for their particular jagged profile is highest, so sorting
patterns reveal relative strengths vs AI.
Formally (under Assumption 2) the bid is AFFINE in the profile:
b_f(y) = r + beta^f . (y - y_M) — a per-dimension price beta_d for each
task skill and beta_* for coordination. the bid of Every firm plane passes
through the common AI anchor (y_M, r): a worker identical to the AI is
worth exactly the rental of one copy cost. A profile lying in the winning of no firm region falls back to the outside option.
# Concept: Technology effect vs re-organization effect (wage decomposition)
Source: Paper 001, §6.3 (Proposition 6).

Along a smooth path of AI improvement, a wage change breaks into parts as:
  the change in the wage as AI improves = technology effect + re-organization effect.
Technology effect: AI capability changes, holding of the firm that uses it, the
size, machine share and human mix FIXED — the value of the human inside the
existing firm. Re-organization effect: the own of the firm adjustments — change
in size (dm), machine share (ds), and human skill mix — each
times the sensitivity of the bid of the firm to that margin.
Key point: equilibrium maximizes total surplus, not wages, so the envelope
theorem does NOT cancel the re-organization terms — they are first-order and
can REVERSE the technology effect. Worked illustration: as firms grow
(m = 2 -> 118) a human wage of the coordinator first RISES with re-organization,
then falls; holding firm composition fixed it would have fallen
in one direction only. Any wage forecast that holds organizations fixed is, in
this model, simply wrong-signed in some patterns.
# Concept: AI capability patterns
Source: Paper 001, §§2.3 (for showing map), 4.3 (formal definitions).

For showing map (§2.3), in (task competence, coordination) space:
- Minimal AGI: matches/exceeds AVERAGE standalone human competence and
  coordination, beats no best-human outright, below the coordination
  threshold.
- Beyond the best single profile: exceeds every human on both tasks, coordination at or
  below threshold.
- Above the line in working together: crosses the coordination threshold, does not exceed
  the best human on every task.
- ASI: exceeds every human on all tasks AND crosses the threshold.

Formal patterns (§4.3) are stated against the human capability HULL:
- Weak: AI is dominated by the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Minimal AGI: AI lies WITHIN the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Superintelligent: AI dominates the convex hull of human capabilities.
- Beyond the best single profile (formal): y_d^M > max_i y_d^i in every productive domain.
- Above the line in working together (formal): gamma^M = gamma(delta_{y_M}) > 1 — an
  ABSOLUTE property of the scale of the AI tail, NOT a comparison against the
  best human coordinator; the average product of an AI-only firm then grows
  without bound in firm size.
the empirical of the paper read of the present (§7.2): current AI is jagged-
high-competence, below the line-coordination — consistent with leaner
startups, less tall org charts, rising demand for experienced human
managers, and little aggregate employment/market-structure movement.
# Concept: Measurement agenda — what to track instead of benchmark scores
Source: Paper 001, §8 (Conclusion).

If transformation is a coordination event, isolated-task benchmarks are a
misleading gauge of it — in both directions (mosaic human teams can beat a
benchmark-winning model today; a benchmark-invisible coordination gain can
tip an economy tomorrow). the proposed of the paper measurements:
1. Scale curves: how output scales with the number of identical AI copies
   at FIXED compute per copy, alongside isolated competence L — estimate
   competence and coordination separately across model generations.
2. Firm-level scale counted in BOTH workers and rented agentic compute
   (m_f = human rolls + AI instances), and payroll-vs-AI-expenditure shares
   over time.
3. The cross-of a section distribution of firms — AI-only / AI-native vs mixed
   — because within-firm vs across-firm displacement have vastly different
   unemployment dynamics.
4. Employer-employee panels tracking how different skill profiles sort
   across firms — the direct test that separates technology from
   re-organization effects on wages.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 002): a second, independent reason benchmark
scores give the wrong idea — they measure a model+HARNESS bundle (scroll 1.2.15).
Paper 002 shows the harness term alone is worth 35-61% of held-out cost
at fixed weights, and that the bundle can keep improving DURING the
measurement task itself. Any competence estimate that does not fix and
report the harness is mixed up.

Revision 2026-11-06 (Paper 003): a worked example of measuring at the
right level. StoryScope measures DISCOURSE-level choices (scroll 1.2.24)
rather than output scores or surface style — and the level matters:
surface style signals are transient (a model revision or a light edit
wipes out them; fine-tuning to mimic human style drops style-based
detection from 97% to 3%, as cited therein), while structural choices
survive style editing (93.9% macro-F1 after span-level rewriting)
because changing them requires rewriting the construction of the artifact.
Measurement lesson for this agenda: prefer variables that are expensive
for the measured system to fake — the same property Paper 001 wants
from scale curves and sorting panels, and Paper 002 gets from
cost-annotated histories (scroll 1.2.20).
# Concept: Harness and harness policy
Source: Paper 002, §§1-2. NEW in Paper 002; no counterpart in Paper 001.

Harness: the scaffolding around a language model that (1) assembles the input of the model and (2) executes and observes its actions in an environment
(usually a computing sandbox). Harness POLICY: every programmable rule
governing how the agent uses model, state and environment — tool
implementations and selection, prompting, context assembly and
pressing together, memory and skill indexing and retrieval, service adapters,
task scheduling — AND the strategy for deciding when and how to attempt
further improvements. Policy is encoded in harness code, prompts,
configuration and reusable skills. Paper 001's agents had capability
profiles but no harness layer; this scroll adds the missing layer
between weights and outcomes.
# Concept: Runtime self-improvement — the collapsed optimization loop
Source: Paper 002, §§1, 3.1. NEW in Paper 002.

Classical setup (adaptive control): an external OPTIMIZER observes
execution and updates the controller (harness policy) between runs —
Meta-Harness, GEPA, AlphaEvolve-style loops. It needs representative
benchmark tasks (75% of production teams in one cited study evaluate
without any), pays full task runs per candidate (untenable as
horizons stretch to days/weeks), benchmarks go stale, and the resulting
policy stays FIXED during the work where its gaps appear.
Collapsed loop: the running agent itself chooses both task actions and
policy revisions through the same action interface, during the task,
grounded in the environment it actually works in. Weights never change.
Each successor policy retains the ability to revise itself further,
including revising its improvement strategy — improvement of the
part that improves is in scope, not a meta-level add-on.
# Concept: Optimizer symmetry and being written in the same stuff it works on (enabling properties)
Source: Paper 002, §3.1. NEW in Paper 002.

Two properties of harnesses make the collapsed loop (scroll 1.2.16)
possible where traditional adaptive control keeps the optimizer outside:
- Optimizer symmetry: a policy update can be REALIZED as a sequence of
  ordinary task actions — inspect source, edit files, run tests, request
  activation — using tools the agent already has. Revision is not a
  privileged operation; it is work.
- Being written in the same stuff it works on: the policy being optimized is code, represented as files
  inside the own of the optimizer environment. The object of optimization is
  available as data to the optimizer.
Together: the proposal of the optimizer logic can move inside the harness
because nothing about proposing a revision requires leaving the action
domain or the representation the agent already works on.
# Concept: Policy/mechanism partition (protected mechanism layer)
Source: Paper 002, §§1, 4.1, after Exokernel (Engler et al. 1995). NEW.

The architectural thing needed first for safe self-editing: partition the
harness into (a) a minimal PROTECTED MECHANISM layer — only what the
safety properties need, exposed at the lowest level of abstraction —
and (b) an editable POLICY layer (the 'the worker part') holding everything
else. Protected: canonical execution history (append-only, queryable,
cost-annotated), versioned artifact store (memory, skills, snapshots),
credential storage, sandbox API, and the guardian (scroll 1.2.19).
Editable: all tools, context assembly/pressing together, prompting, memory
indexing/retrieval, adapters, scheduling — and the improvement policy.
Design pressure runs one way: shrink the protected set to the minimum,
maximize what the agent may rewrite. Two failure modes motivate it:
fate-sharing (a faulty edit turns off the agent AND its repair logic) and
state erasure (an edit destroys the experience needed to learn/recover).
# Concept: State and replacement contracts; the guardian; independent rollback
Source: Paper 002, §§3.2, 4.2, App. A. NEW in Paper 002.

State s_t = (d_t durable data, e_t sandbox, pi_t active policy), advanced
only through protected mechanism K. Two contracts:
- Storage contract: execution history is APPEND-ONLY — H(d_t) is a prefix
  of H(d_t+1); records cannot be written over or deleted. Memory and
  artifacts may change current value but must retain prior versions.
- Replacement contract: editing produces an arbitrary candidate pi'
  (possibly crashing); activation requires a GUARDIAN G(s_t, pi') in
  {0,1} that lies OUTSIDE the policy it judges. Default guardian checks
  only build success; richer guardians (LLM-judge inspection, forked-
  environment trials, human approval) are future work in the paper.
Rollback is INDEPENDENT across the three state components (policy,
durable state, sandbox), and history survives rollback — evidence of
failed attempts stays available. Scope limit (App. A): the preservation
proof is conditional on all protected transitions being mediated by K,
and external effects in the world are not that can be run backward by any rollback.
Protection guarantees preservation and recovery authority — explicitly
NOT improved behaviour or continued progress.
# Concept: Internal legibility (cost-annotated history as reward signal)
Source: Paper 002, §§4.3, 7. NEW in Paper 002.

A self-improving agent needs to see itself. Exo exposes execution history
for retrieval and analysis with model-message events annotated by cost,
token counts and cached-token share, plus a reporting tool (spending,
cached input share, repeated tool calls, context composition). Policy
code decides what enters the prompt, but the full annotated record
remains open to looking inside even when compacted out of context. The paper credits
this as a key reason the system works: cost/latency/cache annotations
give DENSE reward signals about efficiency DURING the task, long before
any completion signal arrives. Legibility is also the audit surface:
adaptations are explicit code diffs that operators can inspect, test,
and give the right to — contra continual learning hidden in weights.
# Concept: Specialization cost and spreading of the cost by reuse
Source: Paper 002, §§5.2-5.4. NEW in Paper 002.

Runtime learning has an investment profile: an early SPECIALIZATION COST
(building tools, revising context policy — in the combat run Exo had
spent ~$0.31 at 10 min vs Codex $0.12 / Pi $0.16) buys a lower spend rate
later; cumulative cost crossing points came at 25 min (vs Pi) and 86 min (vs
Codex). Whether the investment pays is governed by REUSE:
- Within-task reuse (long repetitive tasks): pays before completion.
- Across-task transfer (scroll 1.2.23): pays again on repeated objectives.
- Low-reuse work: does NOT pay. On one-shot quests (each completed once,
  distinct steps/items/locations) Exo averaged $0.28 per quest point vs
  Codex $0.24 and Pi $0.21 — the self-improving harness was the EXPENSIVE
  option. Negative result, recorded at full weight: self-editing is a
  bet on recurrence.
# Concept: Intelligence floor for self-improvement
Source: Paper 002, §5.4 ('Limits of adaptation'). NEW in Paper 002.

Access to self-editing helps only if the underlying model can discover
and reuse effective changes. Control experiment: Exo on AppWorld with
gpt-5-mini — three seeds made limited harness edits and did not reuse
their created tools on held-out tasks; total cost and pass rates were
statistically a wash ($6.63 / 59% vs control $6.73 / 55%). The authors'
statement: 'there is an intelligence floor necessary to reap the
benefits of Of Exo architecture.' The architecture is an amplifier with a
threshold, not a substitute for capability — compare Paper 001's
coordination THRESHOLD (scroll 1.2.7): in both papers the interesting
action is at a critical value of the variable the paper is about.
# Concept: Transfer of learned policy (specialization as portable artifact)
Source: Paper 002, §§5.3-5.4. NEW in Paper 002.

Because adaptations are code, they PERSIST and TRAVEL: a harness
specialized on one run can be carried to a fresh instance of the task
(fresh game account, retained agent state) and pays immediately — the
transfer results are the largest in the paper (45-82% below Codex/Pi;
combat $1.92 vs $6.87/$10.89). Two added conditions from the paper itself:
(1) learned procedures DIFFER across seeds — seeds 1-2 built broadly
reusable tools, seed 3 built many scenario-specific tools and spent more
in learning, yet all three won on held-out cost; specialization does not
converge to a single implementation. (2) Transfer shown is to REPEATED
objectives under changed conditions, not to novel task families.
Relation to Paper 001 scroll 1.2.5: a learned harness is reproducible
like AI expertise generally — but unlike a weight profile, it is also
open to looking inside, that can be forked and that can be owned as an artifact.

Delta-link 2026-11-06 (Paper 004): a SECOND transfer mechanism, unlike
the artifact how well a thing carries above. Paper 004 transfers capability to
unseen tasks with no artifact carried over: a shared pool of expert
networks persists, and a gating network routes each new input to the
experts trained on similar SUBSTRUCTURES (scroll 1.2.32). What travels
is not a learned policy but a routing decision. The two mechanisms make
different bets: artifact transfer assumes the whole specialization is
reusable; substructure routing assumes only parts are, and pays a
gating network to find out which.
# Concept: Discourse-level narrative features vs surface style
Source: Paper 003, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 003.

A generated artifact can be measured at (at least) two levels. SURFACE
STYLE: lexical and syntactic choices — word choice, sentence rhythm,
signature phrases (em-dashes, 'delve'). DISCOURSE / NARRATIVE level:
structural decisions about how the artifact is constructed — plot
architecture, character agency, temporal ordering, how and when
information is revealed, how explicitly themes are stated.

Paper 003's finding: the discourse level carries nearly all the
who wrote a thing signal of the surface level (narrative-only features reach
93.2% macro-F1 on human-vs-AI detection, >=97% of the combined model)
while being far more DURABLE: style cues are erased by model revisions
or light edits, but narrative choices survive span-level style rewriting
(93.9% after editing) because changing them means restructuring the
work, not putting in new words it. The two levels also fail differently: style
identifies THAT a model wrote something; discourse features say HOW
the source constructs — that can be read with sense, per-decision.
# Concept: Narrative space, AI convergence, human dispersion
Source: Paper 003, §§4-5. NEW in Paper 003.

Embed each story as a vector in an induced narrative feature space
(scroll 1.2.28) and the population geometry becomes measurable:
- The five AI sources (Claude, DeepSeek, Gemini, GPT, Kimi) cluster in
  one shared region; the six most-confused attribution pairs are all
  AI<->AI.
- Human stories occupy a DISTINCT region (closest human-AI centroid
  pair farther than the most distant AI-AI pair) and are more DISPERSED
  within it: mean distance to own centroid 33.2 vs 27.4 for AI (+22%),
  median 10-NN radius 1.13x larger.
Convergence here is a population property, not a per-model defect: five
independently trained systems from five labs make systematically
SIMILAR narrative choices (thematic over-being stated out loud, causal being tidy,
temporal straight line behavior, bodily/sensory emotion rendering, vague a pointing at other works without naming them),
while human choices spread wider on the same axes. Relation to scroll
1.2.4 recorded in the 2026 of that scroll-11-06 revision.
# Concept: Core features vs fingerprint features
Source: Paper 003, §§2.2, 4-5. NEW in Paper 003.

A discriminating feature space splits by SCOPE of its signal:
- CORE features (30 of 304): stable, important markers of the
  human-vs-AI boundary that hold across ALL five AI models — signed
  human-leaning or AI-leaning (e.g. thematic being stated out loud and
  of the narrator stating the moral, AI-leaning; chronological discontinuity and
  explicit named between texts reference, human-leaning). 30 core
  features alone reach 84.8% macro-F1 (~91% of the full narrative model).
- FINGERPRINT features (75): signal concentrated in ONE source — the
  odd habits that make six-way attribution possible (68.4% macro-F1
  narrative-only). As reported: Claude = flat event escalation, uniform
  voice, ending chapters, full of respect toward convention; GPT = gossip/rumor as
  plot mechanism, retrospective framing, subverted expectations;
  DeepSeek = front-loaded context; Gemini = most tidy endings, extended
  final endings, most bleak settings; Kimi = fewest fingerprints, sits at
  the generic center of the AI distribution.
General shape worth keeping: a class boundary usually lives in a compact
core; identity within the class lives in a longer tail of fingerprints.
# Concept: Rarity as an originality proxy
Source: Paper 003, §§1, 4-5, App. H. NEW in Paper 003.

Originality is turned into a working measure as STATISTICAL RARITY in a feature space
of decisions: a work is 'rare' by its mean distance to its 25 nearest
neighbors among comparable works (here, stories written to the same
prompts). The legal/theoretical anchor the paper cites: minimal of the copyright-originality standard and Torrance's notion of originality as
being uncommon of response.

Measurements (as reported): human stories' mean rarity percentile 0.71
vs AI 0.49 (Cohen's d = 0.83); humans hold 24.7% of the top-10% rarest
stories vs 7.1% AI (top 1%: 3.0% vs 0.6%); at the prompt level the human
version is the rarest of the six 57.8% of the time (chance: 16.7%).

Watch the limits of the proxy: rarity is relative to the corpus and feature
space chosen; it measures being uncommon of choices, not their quality or
intent; and any rarity metric that becomes a target invites gaming
(Goodhart exposure — see open question Q11).
# Concept: Induced that can be read with sense feature space (comparative induction on a parallel corpus)
Source: Paper 003, §§2, 2.1-2.2. NEW in Paper 003. A METHOD concept —
the first in this model that is about how to build a measuring instrument.

Recipe, as executed:
1. PARALLEL CORPUS: hold the task constant and vary the source —
   10,272 prompts reverse-engineered from human stories, each written
   by the human and five LLMs: 61,608 stories, ~5,000 words each.
   Same-prompt comparison is what makes 'choice' separable from 'topic'.
2. STRUCTURED TEMPLATES: convert each artifact into a structured
   representation along a theory-grounded taxonomy (10 of NarraBench's
   12 narrative dimensions), forcing downstream reasoning over content,
   not surface.
3. CROSS-SOURCE COMPARISON: an LLM judge compares the six versions of
   each prompt in a 600-story discovery pool and records where sources
   systematically diverge (~2.7M tokens compressed to ~686K).
4. FEATURE DISCOVERY + Merging of copies: per-dimension expert prompts propose
   closed-form questions (categorical / ordered / scale / binary /
   multi-select); 408 candidates joined together over 3 runs, embedding-clustered
   (cosine >= 0.85) down to 304 features.
5. ASSIGNMENT AT SCALE: a cheaper model scores every story on every
   feature (Gemini 3 Flash; being the same on a second run Krippendorff alpha = 0.90;
   human validation Cohen's kappa = 0.84 on 240 items).
6. ROLE ASSIGNMENT: bootstrap SHAP stability analysis separates core
   from fingerprint features (scroll 1.2.26).
Total corpus turning into features cost as reported: $4.4k. The transferable
idea: when the interesting variable is a latent CHOICE, don't annotate
it directly — build a mirrored corpus, compress comparisons, and let
the feature space be INDUCED from observed divergence, then validate.
# Concept: Mixture of experts — gating as learned routing over specialists
Source: Paper 004, §§1, 3. NEW in Paper 004 (the architecture is from
Jacobs et al. 1991 and Shazeer et al. 2017; the concept as used here is
Paper 004's deployment of it).

Replace a monolithic network block with several parallel EXPERT
networks plus a GATING network. For each input, the gate computes
weights over the experts (softmax; the weights read as attention), and
the output is the weighted sum of expert outputs — DENSE routing in
Paper 004 (all experts contribute; sparse top-k routing trades
interpretability and stability for compute). Because the gate keys on
the representation of the input, similar inputs are routed to similar
experts, and experts specialize on the regions of representation space
they are routed: specialization is an EMERGENT outcome of routing plus
training, not an assignment. Requirements noted by the paper: experts
sharing a mixture must share input/output shapes (in their graph network, MoE is
applied at the prediction Prediction network, not inside message passing, so experts
share structure); a LINEAR gate worked best in their pre-exploration.
# Concept: Hierarchical (multi-granularity) mixture of experts
Source: Paper 004, §§1, 3-4. NEW in Paper 004.

One mixture is not enough when the object has natural structure at
several scales. Paper 004's object is the control of a program/data-flow
graph, with three natural granularities: NODE (instructions/variables),
BASIC BLOCK (pseudo nodes for code blocks — the level pragma instructions modify),
and GRAPH (the whole kernel). A LOW-LEVEL MoE is built per granularity
(node MoE on post-Prediction network node representations, block MoE on pseudo nodes,
graph MoE on the gathered graph representation). A HIGH-LEVEL MoE then
gates over the three granularity-models' outputs — a mixture of
mixtures, whose gate reads the hidden representation (design 2, better
than gating on raw input features). Two findings carry the concept:
(a) which granularity matters is INPUT-DEPENDENT — different kernels
favor different granularities, and the high-level gate learns to assign
the highest weight to the empirically best granularity per kernel;
(b) stacking the three granularities' MoE models inside ONE flat model is
WORSE than even a single-granularity model (Table 4) — the hierarchy
itself, keeping the mixture of each scale separate and adding up late, is
what trains in a stable way and generalizes. Late aggregation over separately
specialized scales beats early fusion.
# Concept: Expert polarization (mode collapse of a mixture) and its steps taken against it
Source: Paper 004, §§3-4. NEW in Paper 004.

The intrinsic failure mode of a trained mixture: random initialization
gives some experts a head start; the gate learns to weight better
experts higher; better-weighted experts get more training; the gap
compounds until the gate gives nearly everything to ONE expert and
the rest starve — POLARIZATION. The mixture collapses to a single
network, carrying dead capacity, and the generalization the mixture was
built for disappears. Paper 004 shows the collapse concretely at the
high level: naive joint training gives nearly all weight to the
graph-MoE model.
Steps taken against it, both needed (taking one part away to test it Table 6: removing either costs
loss):
1. LOAD-BALANCING PRESSURE: a balance penalty on the coefficient of
   variation of total weights assigned per expert (weight 5e-3 here),
   punishing one sided use.
2. TWO-STAGE TRAINING: stage 1 (warm up) trains each expert model
   SEPARATELY, so every expert is competent before any gating exists;
   stage 2 alternates joint and separate training; the high-level gate
   is initialized to uniform weights. Polarization is a rich-get-richer
   dynamic — the fix is to make experts viable BEFORE competition and
   keep rehearsing them separately during it.
General shape: any routed-specialist system has a collapse gradient;
diversity of the specialist pool is a maintained state, not a default
(cf. Paper 003's convergence at population level, scroll 1.2.25).
# Concept: Domain generalization via shared substructures (routing, not invariance)
Source: Paper 004, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 004.

Problem: a model trained on some domains (here: each program kernel is
a domain) must perform on UNSEEN domains whose difference from the
training domains is large — and INFORMATIVE (the differences carry
signal for the prediction, so erasing them is harmful). The standard
domain-generalization playbook (adversarial alignment, invariant
representations) is therefore REJECTED by the paper: do not force the
domains into one distribution.
The alternative: assume domains share SUBSTRUCTURES even when they
differ wholesale. Learn specialists on substructures (MoE experts over
nodes/blocks/graph patterns) and a router (scroll 1.2.29); a new domain
is handled by composing the specialists its substructures resemble.
Generalization becomes a retrieval-and-composition problem rather than
an invariance problem. In Paper 004 this is what lets a predictor
trained on 42 benchmark kernels transfer to unseen and more complex
kernels with only 50 fine-tuning points per target kernel.
Evaluation protocol worth keeping: leave-one-domain-out — train on
source kernels, test on held-out target kernels; report both offline
prediction error (total mean square error over objectives) and online utility (the
quality of designs the search of the predictor actually finds).
# Concept: Surrogate prediction for expensive design spaces
Source: Paper 004, §§1-2, 4. NEW in Paper 004. The task pattern the machinery of the paper serves.

When evaluating one candidate design costs minutes-to-hours (an high level synthesis
run), exhaustive or even generous search is impossible — so train a
SURROGATE that predicts the expensive quantity (latency, resource use,
validity) from the representation of the design, search cheaply in
prediction space (here: DFS over up to 75,000 pragma designs), and
spend the real evaluation budget only on the predicted top-M (here:
top 10) for verification. the errors of the surrogate are therefore not
uniform in cost: what matters is ranking quality near the optimum and
validity classification at the constraint boundary, which is why the
paper reports ONLINE speedup (best verified design vs best in dataset)
alongside offline mean square error. The binding constraint that shapes everything
else in the paper: labels are scarce BECAUSE they are expensive, so
the surrogate must generalize from few, costly examples (scroll 1.2.32).
# Concept: Fake quadrics — same rational invariants, different manifold
Source: Paper 005, §1. NEW in Paper 005; first concept of the topology
annex (cluster E). No relation to Papers 001-004 beyond meta-level.

A QUADRIC here is S^2 x S^2 (the product of two spheres, a closed
symplectic 4-manifold). A FAKE QUADRIC is a closed symplectic
4-manifold with the SAME rational cohomology ring as S^2 x S^2 —
rationally indistinguishable from it — that is nevertheless not
diffeomorphic (not even homeomorphic) to it: the difference lives in
more fine invariants, here finite nontrivial first homology where the
genuine quadric has H_1 = 0. Akhmedov's families (built in his earlier
work with Baldridge [ref 6 in the paper] by symplectic sums of ruled
surfaces along cyclic multisections, scroll 1.2.35) give INFINITE
families of fake quadrics, distinguished from each other by the order
of the finite group H_1. No complex structure is assumed or claimed:
'fake' is a statement about invariants, not about almost-complex
geometry. The general lesson the concept carries: the identity of an object
is relative to the invariant granularity you measure at — rational
equality is not equality (see also scroll 1.2.38).
# Concept: Cyclic (braided) multisection of a ruled surface
Source: Paper 005, §§1-2, Fig. 1. NEW in Paper 005.

Setting: Sigma_g x S^2, the product of a genus-g surface (g >= 2) with
a sphere — a RULED surface. Fix a map u : Sigma_g -> S^1 (the middle of the sphere circle) whose induced map on pi_1 is onto every target. The CYCLIC
MULTISECTION of degree p >= 2 is the surface
  F_{g,p} = { (x, z) in Sigma_g x S^1 : z^p = u(x) }.
Properties (as stated in the paper): the projection F_{g,p} -> Sigma_g
is a connected, With no merging of sheets p-fold cover (being connected uses
being onto of u_* mod p); F_{g,p} is symplectic for the product
form, has homology class p[S] (S = Sigma_g x {pt}), self-intersection
zero, and genus G = 1 + p(g-1). 'BRAIDED': travelling around a base
loop in a cycle cycles the p sheets (the Figure-1 model, g=2 p=3,
shows the permutation 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1 around a loop of the base) —
the sheets are braided together rather than p separate copies, which
is exactly what makes the cover connected. The name of the construction of the paper: sums of such braided pieces across a sphere seam, the
ring shaped joins carrying no extra braid at the seam itself.
# Concept: Multisection complement and its handlebody decomposition
Source: Paper 005, §§1-2, App. A, Theorem 1.1/2.2. NEW in Paper 005.

The COMPLEMENT M_{g,p} = (Sigma_g x S^2) minus an open tubular
neighborhood of F_{g,p} is the working piece from which fake quadrics
are glued (scroll 1.2.37). Structurally it is a bundle over Sigma_g
with fiber P_p, a sphere with p open disks removed. Paper 005's first
contribution is EXPLICIT HANDLEBODY Recipes in parts of the three
complements the sums need, with 2-handle framings induced by the
product coordinates — handle counts (h_0, h_1, h_2, h_3):
  M_{2,3}: (2, 11, 14, 3)    M_{3,2}: (2, 14, 14, 2)
  M_{2,4}: (2, 12, 18, 4)
A handlebody decomposition turns the complement from an existence
statement into a computable object: its handle chain complex yields
integral homology directly (scroll 1.2.38), its attaching maps are the
data the gluing must match, and (in the announced follow-up with
Akbulut and Yildiz) the closed sums' full diagrams are to be assembled
from these pieces. Proof detail certified in App. D: the pole
projections used to read off framings.
# Concept: Symplectic sum along a surface, and gluing data as real data
Source: Paper 005, §§1, 3-4, Theorem 1.1, App. B. NEW in Paper 005.

The SYMPLECTIC SUM: take two symplectic 4-manifolds containing the
same surface with opposite normal bundles, remove tubular
neighborhoods of the surface, and glue the resulting boundaries by an
orientation-reversing diffeomorphism that flips the normal circle:
phi_m(w, e^{i theta}) = (f_m(w), e^{-i theta}) for w in the genus-G
gluing surface. Gompf's operation; here the pieces are multisection
complements (1.2.36) and the sums are Akhmedov's fake quadrics
(1.2.34). Degree pairs with Euler characteristic 4 and signature 0
from the prior classification: (2,3), (2,4), (3,3); Paper 005 works
the (3,3) and (2,4) families in product ruled surfaces.
The conceptual payload is about DATA vs SUMMARY: Akhmedov-Yeung had
computed the gluing MATRICES on H_1 that the sums require. Paper 005
insists — and this is stated verbatim in its introduction — that 'the
matrices alone do not determine the smooth sums': the actual gluing
requires MARKED SURFACE DIFFEOMORPHISMS f_m realizing those matrices,
with specified boundary choices of plain coordinates (here: no surface-dependent
normal-circle twist). Same matrix, different marking, potentially
different manifold. The invariant summary is a shadow of the gluing
data, not a substitute for it.
# Concept: Integral homology and torsion as the distinguishing invariants
Source: Paper 005, §§2.3, 3.3, 4, Cors 2.4/5.3, App. C. NEW in
Paper 005.

Where Paper 005's computations land. For the complement (Theorem 2.3):
  H_2(M_{g,p}; Z) ~= Z^{1 + 2(g-1)(p-1)} ⊕ Z/p,
and the TORSION subgroup Z/p is generated geometrically (Cor 2.4) by
the difference of the two pole sections, [S_S] - [S_N] — a class with
a picture, not just a chain-complex artifact. For the closed sums
(Theorem 1.1, the integral gluing calculation of §3.3 carried out in §4):
  H_1(Z_m^{3,3}) ~= (Z/3)^2 ⊕ Z/(3m),   H_1(Z_m^{2,4}) ~= (Z/2)^2 ⊕ Z/(4m)
for every twist parameter m >= 1 — recovering, by explicit geometric
realization, the formulas Akhmedov-Yeung had obtained from
Mayer-Vietoris presentations. Full integral homology of the sums
(Cor 5.3): H_0 = H_4 = Z, H_1 = T (the finite group above), H_2 ~=
Z^2 ⊕ T, H_3 = 0; Euler characteristic 4, signature 0 throughout.
The distinguishing logic: rationally all these manifolds are S^2 x S^2
(H_1 vanishes over Q); In whole numbers they are two by two separated by
|H_1| = 27m (in that order 16m) — an infinite family read off one torsion
order. Torsion — the part of homology that the move to fractions deletes —
is precisely where the identity of these manifolds lives.
# Concept: Aspherical sums and automatic symplectic being minimal
Source: Paper 005, §5, Prop 5.1, Cor 5.2. NEW in Paper 005.

Being aspherical: a space is aspherical if its universal cover is
contractible (all higher homotopy groups vanish). Prop 5.1: gluing
two multisection complements by ANY orientation-reversing boundary
diffeomorphism yields an aspherical closed manifold with infinite
fundamental group. Proof shape: each complement has an aspherical
cyclic cover (Sigma_G x P_p, both factors aspherical) with one to one
boundary pi_1, so pi_1 of the sum is an AMALGAMATED PRODUCT over
pi_1(Sigma_G) x Z; the universal cover is then a tree of contractible
pieces over the Bass-Serre tree, and a sphere-mapping argument over
finite subtrees kills all higher homotopy.
Being minimal, for free (Cor 5.2): a symplectic manifold is minimal if
it contains no embedded sphere of self-intersection -1 (no
exceptional sphere to blow down). In an aspherical manifold every
sphere map is shrunk to nothing, so every embedded sphere has square 0 —
a (-1)-sphere cannot exist. Aspherical therefore implies
symplectically minimal in one line. The fake quadrics of Theorem 1.1 are
thus aspherical, minimal, rationally S^2 x S^2, and in whole numbers an
infinite family — all four properties from one construction.
# Concept: Propositions as types — proofs are programs
Source: Study S001, Ch.1 and Ch.4. NEW in Study S001.

A proposition is a type; a proof of it is a term of that type. 't has
type A' and 't proves A' are one claim read two ways (the Curry-Howard
correspondence). Saying 'for all' is a function type whose result may
depend on the input (the Pi type); saying 'if, then' is a plain
function type. A logic is then a reading of a type system: the rules
of the logic and the typing rules pair off, rule for rule (the book
proves the pairing in full, in its Appendix A). What this buys:
checking a proof is the same job as type checking a program, so one
small checker serves both.
# Concept: The proof kernel — one small trusted checker
Source: Study S001, Ch.1, Ch.5, Ch.10, Appendix E. NEW in Study S001.

The kernel is the part of a proof system that checks finished proof
terms against the typing rules. Everything else — tactics, notation,
automation, even a language model proposing steps — only PRODUCES
terms; if the kernel is right, a bug anywhere else can fail to prove
a true thing but can never make the system accept a false one. So
the whole trust of the system is priced into one small part, and the
part is kept small enough to read: in MiniLean4 the kernel is 1,398
lines of Rust with no outside code at all (measured from the open
code, 2026-11-06). Kinship: the guardian of Paper 002 (1.2.19) plays
this role for a running system — a small checker at the gate, all
else untrusted. The difference: the guardian guards state changes;
the kernel guards claims.
# Concept: Typing rules and the judgment
Source: Study S001, Ch.3 and Ch.5. NEW in Study S001.

A judgment is a claim of the form: under this context (an ordered
list of named givens, each with its type), this term has this type.
Eight rules define every judgment: two for contexts (the empty
context; add a name whose type checks), and six for terms (a universe
lives in the next universe; a name has its given type; a Pi type
lands where the imax function puts it (1.2.46); a lambda has the Pi
type of its body; an application puts the input into the result
type in place of the bound name; and conversion — a term may be
read at any type equal to its own by computation (1.2.43)). Seven
of the rules are fixed by the shape of the term; conversion is not,
so the checking algorithm splits in two — Infer computes a type by
shape, Check compares — and conversion is used in exactly one
place. That split is what makes the rules a program.
# Concept: The two equalities — by computation, and as a claim
Source: Study S001, Ch.9 and Ch.12. NEW in Study S001.

Two things are equal in two unlike ways. The first way is by
computation: their terms compute to the same still form (1.3.75) —
the checker decides it by running the computation steps (beta: a
function applied steps into its body with the input put in; delta:
a defined name opens up; iota: a recursor (1.2.44) runs on a built
value). The second way is as a claim: 'a equals b' is itself a
type, Eq, and a proof of it is a term that may need real work
(induction, 1.2.44). Eq is not built in: it is an inductive type
like any other, with one way to build it (refl, when the two sides
are already the same by computation) — and because it has that
shape, its recursor carries a fact about a to a fact about b:
transport, which is how rewriting by an equation works. The example
of the book: n + 0 = n holds by computation (the definition of +
runs on its second input); 0 + n = n does not, and needs induction.
# Concept: Inductive types and recursors
Source: Study S001, Ch.7 and Ch.8. NEW in Study S001.

An inductive type is declared by its ways of being built (its
constructors): Nat is built by zero, or by succ of a Nat. For each
such type the system generates one recursor: a function that takes
a motive (what is to be shown or built for each value), one small
case per constructor, and the value itself — and serves both
recursion (computing a value) and induction (proving a claim) with
the same shape; the case for a built value carries the answers for
its parts (the induction steps for the parts). MiniLean4 gives the
general recipe for any single inductive type (parameters, indices,
many constructors) and the checks a declaration must pass: the
result of each constructor is the type itself, applied to its
parameters in order and to index terms; fields pass strict
positivity (1.2.45); the floors of the fields fit the floor of the
type (1.2.46); and a type living in Sort 0 gives its recursor to
all floors only when it has at most one constructor and every field
is a proof or an index — the rule that lets the recursor of Eq
work everywhere and the recursor of Or only inside propositions.
# Concept: Strict positivity — a type may not eat itself
Source: Study S001, Ch.8. NEW in Study S001.

In a declaration, a field of a constructor may use the type being
declared only in a safe place: either not at all, or as the result
of a function type whose inputs do not use the type. A field that
is a function FROM the type — the type in a negative place — is
refused. The refusal is what keeps out a declaration like 'Bad,
built from a function Bad -> False': that field would let a value
be fed to itself as an input, and the system would prove false
things (the self-eating shape of 1.3.76, one level down). The
check is small (80 lines, measured) because the rule is stated on
the shape of the field types alone.
# Concept: Universes and imax — every type lives one floor up
Source: Study S001, Ch.13. NEW in Study S001.

Sort 0 (propositions) is itself a type, so it must live somewhere:
Sort 0 : Sort 1 : Sort 2, one floor up each time, no floor holding
itself — a single self-holding universe ('Type : Type') proves
every claim (the paradox of Girard, in the form of Hurkens;
1.3.76). A function type takes its floor from the imax function:
if its result is a proposition (floor 0), the whole is a
proposition, no matter what it ranges over — floor 0 is
impredicative, the one floor allowed to range over itself, safe
because proofs there can not be looked inside for data (kin of the
rule of 1.2.44 for when the recursor of a proposition may work at
every floor). Any other result puts the function type at the higher
of the two floors. Real Lean shares this design; MiniLean4 differs
in two stated ways: floors are plain numbers here (one statement
is not made for all floors at once), and proofs are not made equal
by computation.
# Concept: Consistency — the empty claim stays empty
Source: Study S001, Ch.14 and Appendix D. NEW in Study S001.

A logic is consistent when the claim 'for every proposition P, P'
has no closed proof term: a term of that type would prove anything
at all. The book proves it for the core system (the eight rules
with universes, without inductive types) in three moves:
computation keeps types (subject reduction); every well typed term
stops computing (the hard step, taken from the work of others and
named as such); so a closed proof of the empty claim would have to
sit still as a still term, and the shapes a still term can take
leave it nowhere to sit (under the single given P, nothing still
has type P). The honesty of the study is part of the record: the
proof does NOT cover the shipped system with inductive types; for
that the book points to the known results for the calculus of
inductive constructions (Werner 1994). The guarantee of a model is
worth only what its stated scope is — stated here in the canonical
scroll, not in the fine print.
# Concept: Verification by translation — prove the model, and the translator is trusted
Source: Study S002, README and documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022. NEW in Study S002.

To prove a Rust program, Aeneas first carries it into a proof
assistant as a pure functional program (1.2.49, 1.2.50), and the
proofs are proved about THAT. So the claim 'the Rust is right'
stands on a chain, and the study records the links plainly: the
DESIGN of the carrying is proved in papers (the functional
semantics of LLBC, ICFP 2022; LLBC itself proved a sound view of
a heap-and-addresses model of running, and its symbolic semantics
proved a sound borrow checker, ICFP 2024). The CODE that does the
carrying — Charon at the front, Aeneas itself (77,012 lines of
OCaml, measured) — is not verified. The hand-written models of
outside code (the standard library and the rest) are trusted as
written. And the floor is the kernel of the proof assistant the
proofs land in (1.2.41). Verification by translation therefore
moves trust; it does not delete it. Its ledger is this scroll.
# Concept: Backward functions — a borrow handed back is a value plus a put-back
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022. NEW in Study S002.

A shared borrow in Rust becomes, in the translation, the plain
value itself; a mutable borrow becomes the value too — the rule
of Rust itself (1.3.81) is what makes this sound: while a mutable
borrow lives, it is the only road to the value, so the value can
travel in its place. The hard case is a function that RETURNS a
mutable borrow: the one who borrowed goes on editing, and at the
end of the borrow the final value must flow back to the owner.
Aeneas gives such a function two outputs: the borrowed value, and
a BACKWARD function — one more pure function that takes the final
value and hands back the updated owners. (The likeness the
documentation itself gives: a lens — the value is its get, the
backward function its put.) Each function is carried on its own,
from its signature alone; a call is met by the pair, never by
opening the body.
# Concept: LLBC — a borrow calculus with no memory in it
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2022 and 2024. NEW in Study S002.

LLBC (Low-Level Borrow Calculus) is the in-between language of
the toolchain: Charon reads the Rust in the form the Rust
compiler holds it (MIR) and writes LLBC; Aeneas reads LLBC. LLBC
is built to be given a PURE meaning: its semantics is value
based — there is no memory in it, no addresses, no arithmetic on
pointers — and ownership based: a borrow is sound when the LOANS
it stands on are honored, checked as a fact about values in the
semantics, not as a rule about lifetimes in the text. The 2024
paper proves the two things that make this more than a hope:
LLBC is a correct view of a low-level model that does have a heap
and addresses, and running LLBC in the symbolic way (1.2.52)
checks borrowing — a program that passes it does not get stuck
when run on the heap model.
# Concept: Failure as a value — ok, fail, div
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo. NEW in Study S002.

Every function the translation makes returns a Result with three
shapes: ok (a value), fail (the run failed: a panic, a sum that
flows over its whole numbers, a read outside a list, a division
by zero), and div (the run never ends). Nothing is hidden under
the floor: to prove a Rust function right you must prove its
translation never answers fail on the inputs you claim, and
either never answers div or is marked as allowed to. A claim
about a function is written as: the function answers ok with x,
and the fact P holds of x — and the checker of the proof
assistant is walked through such claims call by call by a tactic
the toolchain ships for the shape (step).
# Concept: Symbolic execution as a borrow checker
Source: Study S002, documentation of the repo; ICFP 2024. NEW in Study S002.

The translation is made by RUNNING the LLBC, but on stand-ins:
values are symbols, a borrow is a loan written in a symbolic
state, and where calls or the two arms of a choice meet, the
state is gathered into ABSTRACTIONS — bundles of loans from
which the backward functions (1.2.49) are computed. Loops are the
hard meeting place; the 2024 paper adds the JOIN, a way of
merging two symbolic states that keeps the abstraction and the
borrow facts, proved to keep both — and it is the join that let
loops into the toolchain. In the code that comes out, a loop is
either a small helper function that calls itself, or a loop
combinator fed a body that answers 'go on' or 'done'; that it
stops is proved in the backend, or the function is kept partial
and honest about it.
# Concept: The verified kernel and its untrusted shell, at framework scale
Source: Study S003, the manual, roadmap and design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

h5i-app is a web framework with the split of 1.2.41 built in: the
holder of the application logic is a small KERNEL written in
Rust, and everything around it — the web server, the database,
the decoding of what comes in — is the SHELL, trusted with
nothing. The kernel is carried into Lean 4 (by Charon and Aeneas,
1.2.48, driven as tools under the command of h5i itself) and
properties are proved of it there: that one tenant can not read
the rows of another, that the permission rules hold, that the
state keeps its invariants. For the shapes that come back again
and again — schemas, permission rules, storage — the
specifications and the proofs are made by the tool, not written
by hand. The split is stated in the crate at the center: 'the
core contract between a verified h5i-app kernel and its
untrusted shell'.
# Concept: Receipt before bytes — the record is written before the act
Source: Study S003, the roadmap and browser design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

The red-team half of h5i drives a browser through a proxy that
sits on the road every byte takes, and its rule is: for each
request, a receipt is written FIRST — what was asked, under which
policy, allowed or not — and only then do the bytes move. If the
receipt can not be written, the request does not go. The record
is therefore not a log made after the fact, to be trusted or not;
it is a gate the act itself must pass. The claim of the design,
in its own words, is that tools which drive a browser from
outside 'can not tell you what it reached'; owning the road is
what makes the evidence whole. The pattern is the guardian of
Paper 002 (1.2.19) turned outward: check and record BEFORE the
state changes — here, before the world outside changes.
# Concept: Mutation-tested proofs and the assumption ledger
Source: Study S003, the design files on trust and the app road map. NEW in Study S003.

A proof can be true and still prove nothing you care about, if
its statement was written too weak. h5i answers in two parts.
First, the reach of the verification is governed by written
documents that have the standing of scope authority: what is
proved, what is assumed, what is deliberately not claimed — an
ASSUMPTION LEDGER kept beside the proofs. Second, the proofs are
MUTATION-TESTED: the tool changes the kernel in small wrong ways
(the command is 'h5i app mutate') and checks that the proofs now
FAIL. A proof that still passes a wrong kernel was not holding
the kernel at all. Together: the ledger says what the proofs
mean, and the mutations say whether they bind.
# Concept: Fail-closed boxes for agents
Source: Study S003, the sandbox design files of the repo. NEW in Study S003.

h5i runs agents — its own browser work, and coding agents such as
Claude and Codex — inside BOXES: layers of holding-in (Landlock,
seccomp, separate name spaces for the system tables, and
containers that need no root) with a policy that is FAIL-CLOSED:
a box that can not be set up as specified does not run at all, in
place of running weaker than specified. The box images ship for
the purpose of holding agents, which makes the box the mirror of
the kernel pattern: the agent is an untrusted producer (1.2.41),
the box is the checked boundary it can not talk its way out of.
# Foundations — the level-zero layer (charter rule 9)
Built 2026-11-06 at Will's direction: everything in research.phext is
written so a reader with NO prior specialized knowledge can follow it.
This section is where the base definitions live, sucked in once,
defined in plain words. Rules of the layer:
- A foundation scroll defines ONE term (or one tight family).
- It may use only everyday words, or terms defined in foundation
  scrolls it points to under 'Builds on'. The chains bottom out at
  ordinary language: number, shape, space, same/different, more/less,
  rule, choice, cost. Those are assumed; nothing else is.
- Paper chapters and concept scrolls do NOT contain these definitions.
  Section 3 of each chapter (its standoff references) lists the
  foundation coordinates its claims need, in reading order. Follow
  the coordinates like hyperlinks.
General primitives: 1.3.2-1.3.8. Mathematics: 1.3.9-1.3.27.
Economics: 1.3.28-1.3.32. Computing & agents: 1.3.33-1.3.36.
Language & measurement: 1.3.37-1.3.39. Machine learning & hardware:
1.3.40-1.3.45. DEFINED WORDS (rule 10): from 1.3.46 on, one scroll per
word this phext coins — each scroll written in Singular words alone,
so the word it defines is the only word in it that is not already
Will's. All other scrolls may use a coined word once its scroll
stands here. Added with Study S001 (2026-11-06): foundations
1.3.73-1.3.78 — types and terms; context and judgment; computation
steps and still forms; paradox; universes; tactics and goals. The
Defined Words register grows by use: words coined after the first
register stand at 1.3.79 on (recursor, impredicative). Added with Studies
S002-S003 (2026-11-06): foundations 1.3.81-1.3.83 — ownership and
borrowing; symbolic execution; a tenant — and the coined word
tenant at 1.3.84.
# Foundation: function, variable, input, output
A FUNCTION is a rule that takes an INPUT and produces an OUTPUT:
given the same input, always the same output. A VARIABLE is a named
slot that can hold different values in different situations (x can be
3 today and 7 tomorrow; the rule using x does not change). A PARAMETER
is a variable whose value is chosen once to set up a situation, then
held fixed while other things vary. Nearly every claim in this phext
is, underneath, a claim about some function: what goes in, what comes
out, and how the output changes when the input does.
# Foundation: set and element
A SET is a collection of things, with no order and no repeats: the
set of the three colors on a flag. An ELEMENT is one thing in the set.
Much of mathematics is: pick a set, put structure on it (ways to
combine, compare or measure its elements), then ask what must be true
of everything with that structure. When a definition here says 'a set
with...', it means exactly that recipe.
Builds on: (everyday language only)
# Foundation: 'the same' — equal, isomorphic, homeomorphic, diffeomorphic
'Being the same' in mathematics is always being the same UP TO some allowed way of
reshaping, and arguments are often about which level is meant:
- EQUAL: literally the same object.
- ISOMORPHIC: the same structure — elements can be renamed so every
  rule and relationship matches exactly. (level of the Algebra.)
- HOMEOMORPHIC: the same shape if you may stretch, bend and squash,
  but not tear or glue. A coffee cup and a donut are homeomorphic
  (one hole each). (level of the Topology.)
- DIFFEOMORPHIC: homeomorphic, and moreover smoothly — no corners or
  kinks created or destroyed. The finest of these levels; two spaces
  can be homeomorphic but not diffeomorphic, and 4-dimensional space
  is where that gap gets strange (Paper 005 lives near this gap).
Builds on: set (1.3.3), shape (everyday).
# Foundation: invariant
An INVARIANT is a measurement you can make of an object that does not
change when the object is reshaped in the allowed ways (see 1.3.4).
Invariants are how being the same is tested in practice: if two objects
have different values of an invariant, they are NOT the same at that
level. The trap — it is Paper 005's whole subject — is that equal
invariants do NOT prove being the same: a weak invariant can agree while a
more fine one disagrees. Choosing an invariant is choosing how closely you
are willing to look.
Builds on: 'the same' (1.3.4).
# Foundation: integers, fractions, finite, infinite
INTEGERS (written Z) are the whole numbers: ..., -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, ...
Fractions (written Q) are fractions of integers: 3/4, -7/2, 5 (= 5/1).
Every integer is a rational; most fractions are not integers.
Measuring with Z keeps the fine print (exact counts, leftovers,
what is left over); measuring with Q makes smooth it away, because division is
always possible. A set or group is FINITE if it has a definite count
of elements, INFINITE otherwise. Paper 005's punchline, in these
terms: two manifolds can agree completely under Q-measurement and
differ under Z-measurement — the difference lives in finite leftover
structure (torsion, 1.3.13) that fractions erase.
Builds on: set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: vector and distance
A VECTOR is a list of numbers of fixed length: (3, 1, 4) is a vector
with three entries, called its COORDINATES or dimensions. Vectors let
a complicated thing be pinned down by several measurements at once —
a person's (height, weight, age) is a vector. Once things are vectors,
they live in a SPACE where DISTANCE makes sense: two vectors are
close if their entries are close. A huge amount of this phext works
by turning things (agents, stories, program parts) into vectors and
then asking about distances, clusters and averages of vectors.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: average, spread, percentile, distribution
Given many measurements: the AVERAGE (mean) is their sum divided by
their count. The SPREAD (dispersion, variance) is how far the values
typically sit from the average — same average, very different spreads
is a common and crucial situation. A PERCENTILE places one value
among the rest: the 71st percentile sits above about 71% of the
values. The DISTRIBUTION is the whole picture — which values occur,
how often. Claims like 'group A is more spread out than group B' are
claims about distributions, not about any individual member.
Builds on: (everyday counting).
# Foundation: dimension and manifold
The DIMENSION of a space is how many coordinates you need to say
where a point is, locally: a line is 1, a sheet of paper is 2, the
room you are in is 3. A MANIFOLD is a space that LOOKS like ordinary
flat space of some dimension if you zoom in far enough, even if its
global shape is curved or closed — the surface of the Earth is a
2-dimensional manifold: every small patch looks like a flat map,
though the whole is a sphere. Paper 005 is about 4-dimensional
manifolds: spaces that locally need 4 coordinates. Nobody can picture
one directly; the field works by cutting them into pieces that CAN be
handled (1.3.20) and keeping careful algebraic accounts (1.3.13).
Builds on: vector/coordinates (1.3.7), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: sphere, surface, genus, torus
A SPHERE S^2 is the surface of an ordinary ball: all points at one
fixed distance from a center. (S^1 is a circle, S^0 is two points.)
A SURFACE is a 2-dimensional manifold (1.3.9). Its GENUS is its
number of holes/handles: a sphere has genus 0, a donut (a TORUS) has
genus 1, a three handled shape has genus 3. Sigma_g means 'the surface of genus
g'. Genus is the oldest invariant in the subject: two closed surfaces
with different genera are never the same shape, and genus can be
counted from any picture of the surface.
Builds on: dimension and manifold (1.3.9), invariant (1.3.5).
# Foundation: group
A GROUP is a set equipped with one operation that combines two
elements into a third, satisfying three plain requirements: combining
is order-stable (brackets don't matter), there is a do-nothing element
(identity: 'add 0'), and every element has an undoing (inverse: 'add
the negative'). Examples: the integers under addition; the rotations
of a square under 'do one then the other'; the ways to shuffle a deck.
Groups are how mathematics packages 'symmetry' and 'movement'. A
FINITE group has with a finite count many elements; its ORDER is how many. 'Z/n'
is the group of clock arithmetic with n hours: counting wraps around,
so n steps bring you back to the start — that wrapping around is the seed
of torsion (1.3.13).
Builds on: set (1.3.3), integers (1.3.6).
# Foundation: loops, fundamental group, universal cover, contractible
Stand on a space and walk a closed LOOP: start somewhere, wander,
return to the start. Two loops count as 'the same' if one can be slid
continuously into the other without leaving the space. Loops can be
combined (walk one, then the other) and undone (walk it backwards) —
so the loops of a space form a group, its FUNDAMENTAL GROUP, written
pi_1. pi_1 measures the hole of the space-structure as seen by paths: flat
land has trivial pi_1 (every loop slides to a point); the pi_1 of a donut
is Z x Z (loops around the two hole directions). A space is
CONTRACTIBLE if it can be continuously shrunk to a single point —
contractible spaces have trivial pi_1 and trivial homology (1.3.13).
Every space has a UNIVERSAL COVER: a contractible-ish space laid over
it like an opened out map, whose own pi_1 is trivial; the original
space's pi_1 is then visible as the symmetries of the unrolling.
Builds on: group (1.3.11), manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: homology — holes, counted by algebra; torsion
HOMOLOGY is a machine that gives to each space a list of groups
(H_0, H_1, H_2, ...), designed so that H_k detects 'holes of
dimension k': H_0 counts separate pieces; H_1 counts independent
tunnels (loops that can't be filled in); H_2 counts enclosed voids.
On a donut: H_1 = Z^2 (two tunnel directions, each usable any whole
number of times). The small raised mark-style part Z^n ('free' part) counts
unbounded directions. But an H_k can also contain a FINITE piece, like
Z/3: a cycle that is not a boundary, yet THREE copies of it are. That
finite leftover is called TORSION. Torsion is invisible if you only
measure with fractions (over Q it vanishes, since 3 becomes
that can be undone) — which is why integral (Z) homology knows strictly more
than rational (Q) homology. Paper 005's manifolds are told apart by
torsion alone.
Builds on: group (1.3.11), integers vs fractions (1.3.6), loops (1.3.12).
# Foundation: cohomology and ring
COHOMOLOGY is record of the homology keeping twin: instead of cycles in the
space, it tracks measurement-functions on the space (ways to assign
numbers to cycles consistently). For most counting purposes it holds
the same information as homology. Its extra feature is a
MULTIPLICATION: two measurements can be combined into a third, making
the cohomology groups into a RING (a system where you can add and
multiply, like ordinary numbers). 'The cohomology ring of S^2 x S^2'
therefore names a specific little multiplication table — and Paper
005's manifolds reproduce that table exactly under rational
measurement while differing in their integral homology. Same ring,
different torsion: that is the whole trick of a fake quadric.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), group (1.3.11).
# Foundation: Euler characteristic and signature (two summary numbers)
The EULER CHARACTERISTIC, written chi, is a single integer summarizing
the shape of a space, computable from any decomposition into pieces:
chi = (pieces in even positions) - (pieces in odd positions) — for a
surface, chi = 2 - 2 x genus (sphere 2, torus 0, genus-2 surface -2).
It is additive: the chi of a glued space is the sum of the chi values of the
parts. The SIGNATURE, written sigma, applies to 4-dimensional
manifolds: it counts, roughly, the balance between 'positive' and
'negative' directions in how surfaces inside the manifold cross
each other (the intersection form); sigma is
also additive under careful gluing (Novikov the adding rule). chi = 4 and
sigma = 0 are the values of S^2 x S^2 — Paper 005's sums match both,
as they must to fake the quadric.
Builds on: genus (1.3.10), invariant (1.3.5).
# Foundation: maps between spaces — continuous, embedding, diffeomorphism, orientation
A MAP from one space to another gives each point of the first a
point of the second. A map is CONTINUOUS if nearby points land nearby
(no teleporting, no tearing). An EMBEDDING is a continuous map that
places the first space inside the second without self-intersection —
a knot sitting in ordinary space is an embedded circle. A
DIFFEOMORPHISM is a smooth two-way map (it and its reverse create no
corners or kinks): the notion of exact being the same for smooth manifolds
(1.3.4). An ORIENTATION is a consistent choice of 'which hand it favors'
(clockwise vs counter-clockwise) across a space; gluing two pieces
together requires their orientations to DISAGREE at the seam
(orientation-reversing), the way two halves of a zipper face opposite
ways to close.
Builds on: 'the same' (1.3.4), manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: neighborhood, tubular neighborhood, normal direction
A NEIGHBORHOOD of something inside a space is a small region around
it, included in the space. If a surface sits inside a 4-manifold, a
TUBULAR neighborhood is the region of all points very close to the
surface — it has the shape of the surface made thick slightly in the
directions sticking OUT of it. Those sticking-out directions are the
NORMAL directions (at right angles to the surface, in the only sense
available): at each point of a surface inside a 4-manifold there is a
2-dimensional disk of normal directions, which is why the thick layer
is 'surface x disk' and its boundary is 'surface x circle'. Removing
a tubular neighborhood is the standard way to make a clean hole with a
well-defined boundary, ready for gluing (1.3.23).
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), maps (1.3.16), sphere (1.3.10).
# Foundation: bundle, fiber, section
A BUNDLE is a space built by placing a copy of one space (the FIBER)
over every point of another (the BASE), possibly twisting as you move
around the base. A brush: base = the handle, fiber = each hair of the brush.
Formally the total space maps onto the base, and the points over one
base point are one fiber. A SECTION is a choice of exactly one point
in every fiber, made continuously — a rule for picking a hair of the brush over
each point of the handle. Paper 005 uses this twice: the complement
of a multisection is a bundle over a surface whose fiber is a sphere
with holes; and its torsion class is the DIFFERENCE of two sections
(the two 'pole' sections, over the north of the sphere and south poles).
Builds on: maps (1.3.16), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: covering space and sheets
A COVERING space of a space X is a space laid over X so that every
small patch of X has several exact copies above it (the SHEETS), like
a many floored car park over one floor plan. The number of sheets is
the DEGREE of the cover. A cover is With no merging of sheets if every point of X has
exactly the full number of sheets above it (no merging floors). Going
around a loop in X can cycle the sheets — walk downstairs, and you
may come back on a different floor. That permutation behavior is what
'cyclic' and 'braided' mean in Paper 005: its surface is a p-with sheets
cover of the base surface whose sheets are moved in a cycle in a cycle around
certain loops, braiding the sheets into ONE connected surface instead
of p separate copies.
Builds on: maps (1.3.16), loops (1.3.12).
# Foundation: handle and handlebody decomposition
A HANDLE is a standard building block: take a ball and attach a thick
strap (in dimension terms, a lower-dimensional ball glued along part
of its boundary). A HANDLEBODY DECOMPOSITION describes a manifold as:
start with balls (0-handles), attach straps (1-handles), attach
thicker patches (2-handles), and so on, with a diagram recording each
attachment and a number (the FRAMING) recording how each strap is
twisted as it is glued. The counts (h_0, h_1, h_2, h_3) are the ingredient of the recipe list. Why it matters: a decomposition turns an
abstract space into something computable — homology (1.3.13) can be
read off the recipe by arithmetic (1.3.21), and two pieces can be
glued when their recipes share boundary data.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9).
# Foundation: chain complex — the arithmetic under homology
A CHAIN COMPLEX is the record keeping that makes homology computable.
From a handle decomposition (1.3.20), write one group per dimension:
C_3 -> C_2 -> C_1 -> C_0, where each C_k lists the k-handles, and
each arrow (the BOUNDARY OPERATOR) records how handles of one level
attach to handles of the next, with integer coefficients. The golden
rule is 'the boundary of a boundary is zero' (arrows compose to 0).
Homology is then pure algebra: H_k = (things the arrow out of C_k
kills) / (things hit by the arrow into C_k) — cycles modulo
boundaries. Torsion (1.3.13) appears when a cycle is hit only after
multiplication: 3 copies of it bound, but it doesn't. Given a
handlebody diagram, a patient person (or a computer) can compute all
of this by integer arithmetic alone.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), group (1.3.11), handlebody (1.3.20).
# Foundation: symplectic — geometry with an area rule; the symplectic sum
A SYMPLECTIC structure on a manifold is an added piece of geometry:
at every point, a consistent rule for measuring the 'area' of tiny
tiny flat shapes in pairs of directions — the mathematical form of the
position/momentum pairing in physics. Spaces carrying it are called
symplectic manifolds; they are always even-dimensional, and the rule
makes area-like quantities behave with no give (you cannot squash a
symplectic ball through a small hole, however you bend it). Most
4-manifolds in Paper 005 are symplectic. The SYMPLECTIC SUM is a
cut-and-paste construction: two symplectic manifolds containing the
same surface (with opposite normal directions, 1.3.17) have the neighborhoods of the surface removed and the resulting boundaries glued —
the splicing is done so the area rules still match. It is the main
known factory for exotic symplectic 4-manifolds.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), neighborhood (1.3.17), boundary (1.3.23).
# Foundation: boundary of a manifold; gluing along a boundary
Some manifolds have an EDGE: points whose neighborhoods look like
half-space instead of full space (the edge of a disk is its rim circle; the edge of a ball is its spherical surface). The edge itself is a manifold
of one dimension less, the BOUNDARY, written dM. Removing something's
tubular neighborhood (1.3.17) creates boundary where none was. Two
manifolds with the same boundary (up to being the same, 1.3.4) can be
GLUED: identify the boundaries by a chosen map, and the result is a
new closed manifold. The choice of gluing map is real data — different
choices can yield different manifolds from identical pieces, which is
why Paper 005 specifies its gluing maps explicitly instead of just
their effect on homology.
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), neighborhood (1.3.17), maps (1.3.16).
# Foundation: aspherical; exceptional spheres and being minimal
A manifold is ASPHERICAL if its universal cover (1.3.12) is
contractible — which is the same as saying, it has no higher-homotopy 'bubbles': any
map of a sphere into it can be shrunk to nothing. Its whole topology
is then carried by pi_1. An EXCEPTIONAL SPHERE is an embedded sphere
whose self-intersection number is -1 (roughly: it links itself
negatively once); such a sphere can always be 'blown down' (removed
by a standard surgery), so a symplectic manifold containing none is
called MINIMAL — it is not a blown-up version of something smaller.
Paper 005's one-line consequence: in an aspherical manifold every
embedded sphere has self-intersection 0, never -1, so aspherical
implies minimal.
Builds on: universal cover (1.3.12), embedding (1.3.16), symplectic (1.3.22).
# Foundation: gluing groups — amalgamated product and trees of spaces
When two spaces are glued along a shared part, their fundamental
groups combine in a forced way: the AMALGAMATED PRODUCT takes both
groups and identifies the two copies of the shared group of the part
inside them (like merging two organizations while holding that the
shared department is one department, not two). The combined group
acts on a TREE (the Bass-Serre tree) whose vertices are copies of the
pieces and whose edges are copies of the shared part — a floor plan
of how the glued space's universal cover is assembled from
contractible blocks. Normal forms in such groups say every element
has an essentially unique spelling as an alternating product from the
two sides, which is what lets proofs track elements across the seam.
Builds on: fundamental group (1.3.12), group (1.3.11).
# Foundation: two computing tools — Mayer-Vietoris and Poincare duality
MAYER-VIETORIS is the standard divide-and-conquer for homology: if a
space is two pieces overlapping in a known part, there is a
prescription (an exact sequence) that computes the homology of the
whole from the homology groups of the pieces and the overlap — the
topological version of inclusion-exclusion in counting.
POINCARE DUALITY is a symmetry of closed manifolds: in dimension n,
the k-th homology and the (n-k)-th cohomology determine each other.
Consequence used in Paper 005: if H_1 is finite, duality forces H_3
of a closed 4-manifold to vanish, because H_3 is tied to cohomology in
degree 1, and finite groups contribute nothing there.
Builds on: homology (1.3.13), cohomology (1.3.14).
# Foundation: contact manifold and Stein filling (brief)
A CONTACT structure on an odd-dimensional manifold is a field of
planes with maximum twisting — the geometry of wave waves and of
thermodynamics' special curves; a 3-manifold with one is a contact
3-manifold. A STEIN FILLING of it is a particularly well-behaved
4-dimensional manifold whose boundary is that contact manifold — a
filling the way a solid fills a shell. Paper 005 mentions them
because its complements, with one more piece removed, become Stein
filling spaces — including infinitely many that share ALL ordinary homology
yet are two by two different manifolds, told apart only by looking at
homology of their finite covers (1.3.19 run in reverse).
Builds on: manifold (1.3.9), boundary (1.3.23), homology (1.3.13).
# Foundation: firm, worker, wage, outside option
A FIRM is an organization that hires inputs (labor, materials,
compute) and sells outputs. A WAGE is the price of the labor of a worker
per unit time. the OUTSIDE of A worker OPTION is the best they could get
if they leave — the wage they could earn elsewhere, or the value of
not working. No firm can hold a worker for less than their outside
option, and a worker whose skills are scarce gets paid above it —
the excess being a rent (1.3.31). Paper 001 treats AI systems rented
by the instance as a kind of worker whose wage is its rental cost.
Builds on: (everyday language; function (1.3.2) for prices as signals)
# Foundation: equilibrium
An EQUILIBRIUM is a situation in which every participant, given what
everyone else is doing, is doing the best they can — so nobody has
reason to change on its own side alone, and the situation rests. A market
equilibrium adds: at the resting prices, the amounts people want to
buy equal the amounts people want to sell (the market CLEARS).
Equilibrium is a consistency condition, not a claim that the outcome
is good, fair, or efficient — though theorems sometimes connect them.
Paper 001's results are equilibrium statements: 'in equilibrium, the
wages must be...' means 'any other wage pattern would give someone a
profitable move'.
Builds on: firm/wage (1.3.28).
# Foundation: mixing and the convex hull
Take points in a space. A MIX (convex combination) is a weighted
average of points: weights are positive and sum to 1, like a recipe
in proportions. The set of ALL mixes of a collection of points is
their CONVEX HULL — the smallest 'straight-edged' region containing
them all; picture a rubber band snapped around pegs on a board. If
the pegs are people's skill profiles (1.3.7), the hull is every
average profile a team drawn from those people can have, because the average of a team is exactly a mix of its members. Whether the best
mix sits strictly inside the hull or at a corner is the whole
question in Paper 001's mosaic argument.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), average (1.3.8).
# Foundation: scarcity, rent, and the cost of a copy
A resource is SCARCE if there is not enough of it for everyone who
wants it at a zero price. RENT is the payment a scarce resource earns
above what is needed to keep it supplied: a uniquely talented singer
earns rent; an ordinary microphone doesn't, because more can be made.
The dividing line is COPY COST: if a thing can be copied at cost r,
no copy can command more than about r for long, because a competitor
can mint another. If it cannot be copied at any price — a particular
trained of the human brain — its earnings are set by bargaining over
scarcity. Paper 001's first great divide: human expertise is
that can not be copied (fixed supply, rents possible); AI expertise is that can be copied
at compute cost (rents capped by the copy cost).
Builds on: firm/wage (1.3.28).
# Foundation: threshold and phase transition
A THRESHOLD is a value of some quantity at which the behavior of a system
changes in kind, not just in degree. A PHASE TRANSITION is the
physical archetype: water heated degree by degree does nothing
qualitative until 100°C, then boils — the same input, a different
regime. Many claims in this phext are threshold claims: below the
threshold, step by step change; above it, a different structure. A
threshold claim is stronger (and more falsifiable) than 'more X leads
to more Y': it says the interesting action is at one point of the
scale, and it invites the question 'which side are we on, measured
how?'
Builds on: function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: program, runtime, state
A PROGRAM is text that specifies computation. A RUNTIME is the
machinery that carries a program out: loading it, giving it memory
and tools, stepping it forward. STATE is everything about a running
system that can change as it runs — the contents of its memory at a
moment. Two systems running the same program with different state
behave differently; two systems in the same state with different
programs do too. Paper 002's entire subject is the layer of program
that surrounds the fixed of a model core and shapes what its state becomes.
Builds on: function (1.3.2).
# Foundation: policy and mechanism
A POLICY is a rule for choosing what to do in each situation — the
strategy an agent follows. A MECHANISM is the fixed scaffolding that
makes policies run and constrains them: who may act, what gets
recorded, what is checked. The distinction that matters here: a policy
can be wise or foolish, but a mechanism is what remains true even when
a policy is foolish or hostile. Institutions, operating systems and
agent runtimes all make this split somewhere; Paper 002's
contribution is drawing the line sharply and making only one side
self-editable.
Builds on: program/runtime (1.3.33).
# Foundation: sandbox, version, rollback, append-only
A SANDBOX is a inside a fence area where a program can act without touching
the real system outside the fence — experiments inside can't break
things outside. A VERSION is a saved snapshot of some state or text,
kept under a name or number. ROLLBACK is returning to a saved version
after a change goes wrong; it is only trustworthy if the snapshot was
kept outside the reach of the thing being rolled back. APPEND-ONLY
means records can be added but never edited or deleted — a ledger in
ink. Append-only histories are how systems prove what happened, and
the foundation of Paper 002's safety story.
Builds on: state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: token, context, cache (how a language model reads)
A TOKEN is the chunk of text a language model reads or writes at one
step — roughly a short word or word-piece. The CONTEXT is the tokens
the model can currently see: its entire working memory, limited to a
fixed number of tokens (the context window). Work the model did
earlier falls out of view unless something writes it down and brings
it back — that 'something' is memory machinery around the model, and
choosing what to keep in view is a policy (1.3.34). A CACHE is stored
earlier computation reused instead of computed again; for models, a high
share of cached input tokens means most of the context was already
processed, so the step is cheap. Costs in agent systems are roughly
proportional to not cached tokens processed.
Builds on: program/state (1.3.33).
# Foundation: corpus, prompt, feature
A CORPUS is a collection of texts (or other items) assembled for
study. A PROMPT is the instruction or opening given to a writer or a
model, to which it responds with a complete piece of work. A FEATURE
is one measured property of an item, stated so different items can be
compared: 'number of side plots', 'does the narrator address the
reader'. Turning an item into a list of features turns it into a
vector (1.3.7) — after which distances, clusters and rarity (1.3.39)
become arithmetic. What a study can see is bounded by its features:
unmeasured properties are invisible to it.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), set (1.3.3).
# Foundation: classifier and its report card (accuracy, F1)
A CLASSIFIER is a machine (or rule) that sorts items into labeled
bins: given the features of an item, it guesses which source or class the
item came from. Its REPORT CARD compares guesses to truth. Accuracy
is the share of all guesses that are right. F1 is a more strict grade
balancing two failure modes per class: missing real members
(recall) and wrongly claiming members (precision); MACRO-F1 averages
the per-class F1 grades so small classes count as much as big ones.
A classifier far above the guess-at-random rate proves the features
contain real information about the classes — it does not, by itself,
prove anything about why.
Builds on: feature (1.3.37), average (1.3.8).
# Foundation: nearest neighbors, distance, rarity
Once items are vectors (1.3.7), each item has NEIGHBORS: the items
closest to it in the space. The k NEAREST NEIGHBORS of an item are
the k closest such items, and the average distance to them measures
how crowded the neighborhood of the item is. RARITY, as Paper 003 uses it,
is exactly that: an item is rare if its nearest neighbors are far
away — its combination of measured properties is uncommon in the
collection. Rarity is always relative to the collection and the
features: add items or change features and the same rarity of the item
changes.
Builds on: vector (1.3.7), average (1.3.8), feature (1.3.37).
# Foundation: neural network, weights, training
A NEURAL NETWORK is a function (1.3.2) built from many simple units
whose combined behavior is set by a large list of adjustable numbers,
its WEIGHTS (or parameters). TRAINING is the process of adjusting the
weights — by automated trial, error measurement and correction — so
the outputs of the network match desired outputs on training examples.
Training is expensive and slow; using the trained network (inference)
is comparatively cheap. The weights are the entire of the network learned
knowledge, stored as numbers; two networks with the same design but
different weights are different machines. Several papers here hold
weights fixed and vary everything AROUND them — the interesting
action is deliberately elsewhere.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), vector (1.3.7).
# Foundation: graph — nodes, edges, and program graphs
A GRAPH is a set of NODES (things) connected by EDGES (relationships
between pairs of things): cities and roads, people and friendships,
instructions and dependencies. Degree, paths and neighborhoods are
defined as you'd expect. A GRAPH NEURAL NETWORK (graph network) is a neural
network (1.3.40) whose computation follows the edges of a graph, letting
the representation of each node absorb information from its neighbors. For
programs, the standard graph is the control/data-flow graph: nodes
are instructions and values, edges record 'executes next' and 'feeds
into'. Paper 004 predicts hardware behavior from program graphs.
Builds on: set (1.3.3), neural network (1.3.40).
# Foundation: generalization and domain shift
A model GENERALIZES if it performs well on items it was not trained
on. A DOMAIN is a family of similar items (the problems of one benchmark;
one program; the scans of one hospital). DOMAIN SHIFT is what happens when
the test items come from a different domain than the training items:
the surface changes while the underlying task doesn't, and models
often fail badly. Domain generalization is the discipline of building
models whose performance survives the shift. The core dispute in
Paper 004: whether to survive shift by ERASING domain differences
from the view of the model, or by KEEPING the differences and routing each
new item to specialists that recognize its parts.
Builds on: classifier (1.3.38), neural network (1.3.40).
# Foundation: surrogate model
A SURROGATE is a cheap, fast stand-in for an expensive, slow
measurement: predict the result instead of running the real thing.
Wind-tunnel models are stand ins for flight; a spreadsheet budget is
a surrogate for a year of business. A surrogate is useful when its
errors are small compared to the cost it saves, and when the final
decision still checks the few best candidates the expensive way.
Its quality is judged twice: how close its predictions are (offline),
and how good the decisions made from them turn out (online). Paper
004's stand ins predict chip performance in milliseconds where the
real measurement takes hours.
Builds on: function (1.3.2), classifier report cards (1.3.38).
# Foundation: FPGA, kernel, pragma, latency (the high level synthesis stage, plainly)
An FPGA is a chip whose circuits can be rewired after manufacture —
hardware you can reprogram. HIGH-LEVEL SYNTHESIS (high level synthesis) is a tool that
compiles an ordinary program into such circuits. In this vocabulary a
KERNEL is the program (in a confusing way, the same word means other things
elsewhere; here it is a piece of C++ to be turned into circuitry). A
PRAGMA is an instruction written into the program telling the compiler
how to build the circuit: run these steps in parallel, pipeline
those, tile this loop. The same kernel with different pragma instructions becomes
circuits of wildly different speed and size. LATENCY is how long the
circuit takes (in cycles); resource use is how much chip it occupies.
Finding good pragma instructions by trial requires running high level synthesis each time — hours
per trial — which is why Paper 004 predicts instead (1.3.43).
Builds on: program (1.3.33), surrogate (1.3.43).
# Foundation: regularization and fine-tuning (two training habits)
REGULARIZATION is any deliberate pressure added during training to
keep a model from a degenerate solution: a penalty for being one sided,
for complexity, for being too sure — chosen to protect a property the
raw score doesn't reward. Paper 004 adds a balance pressure for BALANCE: experts
that are never used can't specialize. FINE-TUNING is training an
already-trained model a little more, on a small amount of new data,
to adapt it to a new domain — cheap compared to training from
scratch, and the standard first move under domain shift (1.3.42).
Both are admissions that the headline objective alone doesn't capture
what's wanted.
Builds on: training (1.3.40), domain shift (1.3.42).
# Defined Words — the register (charter rule 10)
Words this phext needs that are not in Will's Singular list live here,
one per scroll, each defined in a scroll written in Singular words
alone (the word being defined, and the forms named in its first line,
are the only words in the scroll that are not already Will's). A word
may be used in the rest of the phext because its scroll stands here.
Names need no scroll: they are addresses, not speech.
# Defined word: homology
Homology is the algebra that counts the holes of a space. It gives a space a list of groups. The first group counts the separate pieces. The next counts the tunnels: loops that can not be filled in. The next counts the closed empty rooms inside. A part of the answer can be finite: a loop that is not the edge of anything, though three copies of it are. That finite part is the part that measuring with fractions can not see.
# Defined word: cohomology
Cohomology is the twin record keeping of the hole counting algebra. In place of cycles in the space, it keeps the ways to give numbers to cycles so the numbers agree. It holds the same news as the hole counting algebra for most counting. It adds one thing: two such ways can be multiplied to make a third, so the groups form a ring, a system where you can add and multiply.
# Defined word: symplectic (also: symplectically)
A space is symplectic when it carries an added rule: at each point, a way to measure the area of tiny flat shapes in pairs of directions, the same pairing of place and push that physics uses. The rule is strict: you can bend the space, but the rule makes it impossible to press a ball of the space through a hole smaller than the rule allows. A symplectic sum joins two such spaces along a shared surface so the rules still match.
# Defined word: aspherical
A space is aspherical when every map of a sphere into it can be shrunk to nothing. The covering space that opens all its loops out flat is then a space that shrinks to a point. All of its shape news is carried by its loops alone.
# Defined word: genus
The genus of a surface is its number of holes, counted as handles. The surface of a ball has genus zero. A donut has genus one. A shape with three handles has genus three. Two closed surfaces with unlike genus are never the same shape.
# Defined word: quadric (also: quadrics)
A quadric, in this phext, is the product of two spheres: each point is a pair, one point from the first sphere and one from the second. It is the standard four dimensional space that the manifolds of Paper 005 copy in every test that uses fractions, and fail to be in fact.
# Defined word: multisection (also: multisections)
A multisection is a surface sitting inside a product space, spread over the base surface as a cover with p sheets. The sheets are joined into one surface when travel around a loop of the base cycles them. The multisections of Paper 005 are cyclic: one loop sends sheet one to sheet two, sheet two to sheet three, and the last back to the first.
# Defined word: handlebody (also: handlebodies)
A handlebody is a space given as a building recipe: balls first, then straps, then thicker patches, each joined along part of its edge, with a number for each strap that tells how it twists as it is joined. The list of counts of each kind is the bill of parts of the recipe. From the recipe alone, the hole counting of the space can be worked out by whole number arithmetic.
# Defined word: gluing (also: gluings)
Gluing is the joining of two spaces along a shared edge or surface, by a chosen map that says which point of one side meets which point of the other. The choice of map is real: two gluings of the same pieces by unlike maps can give unlike spaces.
# Defined word: diffeomorphism (also: diffeomorphisms)
A diffeomorphism is a map between two smooth spaces that goes both ways and makes no corners or kinks in either direction. It is the most strict of the same shape tests used here: spaces joined by one are the same smooth space.
# Defined word: diffeomorphic
Two spaces are diffeomorphic when a map joins them that goes both ways and makes no corners or kinks in either direction: they are the same smooth space, point for point.
# Defined word: homeomorphic
Two spaces are homeomorphic when one can be stretched, bent and squashed into the other with no tearing and no joining, and back again. This is a less strict same shape test than the smooth one: spaces can pass it and still fail the smooth test.
# Defined word: contractible
A space is contractible when it can be shrunk, inside itself, to a single point, the way a ball of clay can be pressed down without being torn. Such a space has no holes of any kind.
# Defined word: hull
The hull of a set of points is the smallest straight edged region that holds them all: the shape a rubber band takes around pegs on a board. Every weighted average of the points lies in the hull, and every point of the hull is such an average.
# Defined word: centroid
The centroid of a set of points, or of a cluster, is its center point: the average of all the points, each given the same weight. Distances to a centroid say how spread out the set is around its own middle.
# Defined word: seam
A seam is the surface or line along which two pieces are joined. When two pieces are joined by a chosen map, the seam is where the map does its work, and how the pieces meet at the seam decides what the joined space is.
# Defined word: framing (also: framings)
A framing is the twist number of a strap in a building recipe: as a handle is glued down, it may be turned some whole number of times, and that number is part of the recipe. Unlike framings of the same recipe give unlike spaces.
# Defined word: gating
Gating is the choosing, for each input, of how much weight each of several expert parts will have in the answer. A gate is the small part that makes the choice, after looking at the input. The weights add to one, so the answer is a weighted average of the experts.
# Defined word: polarization
Polarization, in a mixture of experts, is the collapse of the choosing: the gate sends nearly all weight to the one expert that got ahead first, that expert gets nearly all the training, and the gap grows until the mixture is one expert and dead weight. It is a rich get richer failure, and it must be trained against.
# Defined word: supercooperation
Supercooperation is the state of a system of agents whose working together has crossed the line where growing the group makes each added member worth more, not less. Below the line, groups hit a ceiling on their size. Above it, returns grow with scale, and the ceiling breaks.
# Defined word: substructure (also: substructures)
A substructure is a part of a structure that keeps its own pattern: a block of a program, a turn of a story, a piece of a space. Two large unlike things can share substructures, and what is learned on one can be carried to the other through the shared part.
# Defined word: granularity (also: granularities)
Granularity is the size of the parts you choose to look at: the grain. One program can be read at the grain of single steps, of blocks, or of the whole. A claim at one granularity need not hold at another, and some machines keep a separate expert for each grain.
# Defined word: jagged (also: jaggedness)
A profile is jagged when it is strong in one direction and weak in another, and the pattern of strong and weak is its own. Two jagged profiles can fit together like teeth: the weak place of one is the strong place of the other. That fit is the root of the mosaic effect.
# Defined word: coasean
Coasean names the line of thought from Coase: a firm grows until one more step inside costs as much as buying that step in the market. The ceiling this sets on firm size is the coasean ceiling.
# Defined word: ricardian
Ricardian names the line of thought from Ricardo: a thing that can not be copied earns by its scarcity. Its owner can charge above the cost of keeping it in use, and the excess is a rent that copying would destroy.
# Defined word: amalgamated
An amalgamated product is a group built by joining two groups along a shared part: the two copies of the shared part are made into one, and nothing else is forced. It is how the loop group of a joined space is built from the loop groups of the pieces.
# Foundation: types and terms
A TYPE is a kind label: 'a whole number', 'a true-or-false claim',
'a function from numbers to numbers'. A TERM is a thing that
carries one: the number 3, a proof, a function. One set of building
rules makes both — a term is itself a type when its own label is a
universe (1.3.77). The whole language of terms of MiniLean4 has
five kinds: a universe Sort u; a name; a Pi type (for each input of
one type, a result of a type that may depend on the input); a
lambda (a function built from a bound name); and an application
(one term put to another). Paper chapters add one more kind: a
constant, a name declared once for the whole system.
Builds on: 1.3.2 (function, input, output), 1.3.3 (set).
# Foundation: context and judgment
A CONTEXT is an ordered list of givens: names, each with its type,
where a later type may use the names before it. A JUDGMENT is a
claim made under a context: 'under these givens, this term has
this type'. The typing rules (1.2.42) say which judgment claims hold.
A judgment with an empty context is a claim that stands on its
own, with nothing given.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms).
# Foundation: computation steps and still forms
A term COMPUTES by steps of three kinds: beta (a function applied
to an input steps to its body, with the input put in place of the
bound name — putting in must not let a name be caught by a binder
of the same name inside; the system renames first, and getting
this wrong is where checker bugs hide); delta (a name with a
definition opens up to its definition); iota (a recursor (1.2.44)
applied to a built value runs the case for that builder). A term
to which no step applies is STILL (a normal form). Two terms are
equal by computation when both compute to the same still form,
up to renaming of bound names. A working checker puts a cap on
the steps (its fuel; MiniLean4: 20,000) so a bad term stops with
an error in place of running forever.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms), 1.3.4 ('the same').
# Foundation: paradox
A PARADOX is a build inside a system that makes the system prove
a claim and its opposite — after which the system proves anything
and is worth nothing. The paradox of this study: if one universe
held itself ('Type : Type'), a term can be built (Girard, made
smaller by Hurkens) that proves 'for every proposition P, P' —
the empty claim of 1.2.47 filled. The shape of the trick: a
collection that ranges over all types while being one lets it
hold a copy of its own double power set, a thing no set can do;
the load is carried by well-founded families that are, all of
them, below a family that is itself one of them.
Builds on: 1.3.3 (set), 1.3.77 (universes).
# Foundation: universes (floors of types)
A UNIVERSE is a type whose terms are types: Sort 0 holds the
propositions, Sort 1 holds Sort 0 and the small types (Nat lives
here), Sort 2 holds Sort 1, and so on — floors of a building, each
holding the one below, no floor holding itself (a floor that held
itself is the paradox of 1.3.76). To say which floor a function
type lives on, the imax rule: if the result lives on floor 0, the
function type lives on floor 0 too, whatever its input ranges
over; else it lives on the higher of the two floors.
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms).
# Foundation: tactics and goals
A GOAL is a judgment still to be proved: a context, a target type,
and a hole where the proof term will go. A TACTIC is a step that
fills a hole with a term — possibly a term with new holes, which
become new goals. In MiniLean4 the tactics are: intro (the target
is a function type; take its input as a given and prove the rest),
exact (put in a finished term), apply (put in a function and leave
its inputs as goals), change (restate the target as one equal to
it by computation), rfl (close a goal of Eq when its sides compute
the same), induction (run the recursor of a given on the goal),
rw (rewrite the goal by an equation), and qed (send the whole
built term to the kernel). The soundness of the system rests on
qed alone: tactics only propose, the kernel decides.
Builds on: 1.3.74 (context and judgment), 1.3.75 (computation
steps).
# Defined word: recursor (also: recursors)
A recursor is the one function a system like MiniLean4 builds for
each inductive type: it takes what is to be shown or built for
each value of the type, one small case for each way of building a
value, and the value itself, and it walks the value by its
building steps. With a claim as its aim it is induction; with a
value as its aim it is recursion. One shape serves both.
# Defined word: impredicative
A floor of types is impredicative when a type on that floor may
range over the whole floor, itself counted in, and stay on the
floor. In MiniLean4 only floor 0, the propositions, is
impredicative: 'for every proposition P' is itself a proposition.
One such floor is safe; two would let the paradox in, for a type
could then be looked inside as data.
# Foundation: ownership and borrowing
In Rust, every value has exactly one OWNER. A value may be
BORROWED in two ways: shared (many readers at once, no writer) or
mutable (one borrow at a time, and while that borrow lives no
other road to the value exists — not even its owner). When a
borrow ends, the road returns to the owner. A checker in the Rust
compiler (the borrow checker) refuses any program that breaks
these rules, before the program runs. The rules are what Aeneas
(Study S002) stands on: because a mutable borrow is the only road
to its value, the value can stand in for the borrow (1.2.49).
Builds on: 1.3.33 (program, runtime, state).
# Foundation: symbolic execution — running on stand-ins
To run a program SYMBOLICALLY is to run it with stand-ins in
place of its values: where the program would hold the number 3,
it holds 'some whole number, call it n', and where it would
branch, the running follows both arms with the condition of each
written down. The result is not one run but the shape of all
runs. Checking a program this way can find what no set of test
runs reaches; carrying a program into another language this way
is how Aeneas works (1.2.52).
Builds on: 1.3.73 (types and terms), 1.3.2 (function).
# Foundation: a tenant
In a system shared by many customers, a TENANT is one customer's
sealed region: their rows, their files, their names — present in
the same store as those of every other tenant, and kept apart by
the rules of the system. That tenant A can not read the rows of
tenant B is the kind of claim the framework of Study S003 proves
of its kernel (1.2.53): the seal is a theorem, not a hope about
careful code.
Builds on: 1.3.33 (program, runtime, state).
# Defined word: tenant (also: tenants)
A tenant is one customer of a system that many customers share,
counted as a sealed region of the system: the rows, files and
names of the tenant are in the same store as those of every other
tenant, and the rules of the system keep them apart. To say a
system is safe for its tenants is to say no tenant can reach into
the region of another.
# Paper 001 — Organizing Intelligence
arXiv:2611.05417 [econ.TH], v1, submitted 31 Oct 2026.
Alex Imas (Google DeepMind; also U. Chicago), Andrew Koh (Google DeepMind;
also Columbia), Shane Legg (Google DeepMind). Lead authors equal
contribution, alphabetical. 108 pp.; main text to p.53, proofs Appendices
A-E. CC BY 4.0. Views are the authors', not Google's; not a forecast —
outcomes conditional on model assumptions (their footnote 1, repeated).

One-paragraph distillation: Intelligence matters economically through
organizations. Build agents with multidimensional capability profiles,
let them sort from inside itself into competing firms, split capability into
competence (level) and coordination (scaling), give AI reproducible
expertise and possibly unbounded coordination — and equilibrium firm size,
market concentration, employment and wages all become functions of WHICH
capability improves and whether it crosses the coordination threshold.
Canonical concepts: Chapter 1, Section 2 (scrolls 1.2.1-1.2.14).
# Paper 001 — Formal core
§§3-4. Primitives:
- Populations: humans as a fixed measure over capability profiles
  y = (task skills..., y*) in Y; AI as unlimited copies of one profile y_M
  at rental (compute) cost r per copy; human outside option / reservation
  wage w.
- Firm: a measure nu over profiles adding up to size m; output F(m*nu) with
  (CES family in the examples) competence aggregator over mean task skills
  times a scale factor S(m, z*) in mean coordination z*.
- Demand: downstream inverse demand P(Q); firms compete, free entry.
- Equilibrium (§4.1, Theorem 1): an industry structure (distribution over
  firm types) + wage function such that firms break even, no feasible
  entry or re-organization is profitable, markets clear — and, by Theorem 1,
  equilibrium outcomes are EFFICIENT (maximize total surplus) with wages
  supported as shadow values / maximal bids. Existence and the efficiency
  equivalence are the Theorem of the paper 1; Appendix A collects proofs.
- Patterns of AI capability are defined in §4.3 (canonical scroll 1.2.13).
# Paper 001 — Worked example (§2), the numbers
Economy: 14 humans — 6 coders (9,1; y*=0.75), 6 writers (1,9; y*=0.75),
2 coordinator profiles (1,1; y*=0.97). Outside option w = 0.25; AI copies cost r = 1.

- Coders alone: solo competence 1.8; all 6 in one firm at preferred size 6
  produce 5.0/worker. Double the coders and they SPLIT into two firms of 6
  (59.5 total) rather than one of 12 (57.2) — coordination caps size.
  Scarce coders earn 1.49, ~6x outside option.
- Add writers (mosaic): balanced coder-writer firms reach competence 5
  (mean profile (5,5,0.75)); two firms of 6 produce 13.8/worker, 165.3
  total, beating siloed firms (59.5) and one merged firm (158.9). the wage of Every specialist nearly sets of three alike ones to 4.13 with zero change in their own
  skill — pure fitting together rent.
- Add coordinator profiles: standalone-weakest workers (L = 1, solo wage 0.70).
  In teams they lift mean coordination, preferred size rises 6 -> 7,
  output 13.0/worker in two 7-person firms (182.0 total) still beats one
  14-person firm (175.7). Coordinator profiles earn 2.41, ~10x outside option —
  management softens but does not remove the Coasean ceiling (y-bar* < 1).
- AI patterns are then compared on this economy (canonical scroll 1.2.13);
  the paper notes today's multi-agent AI coordination evidence is poor:
  41-87% task failure from inter-agent being out of line (Cemri et al. 2025),
  adding agents cutting sequential-planning performance 39-70%, error
  amplification up to 17.2x in with no working together swarms.
# Paper 001 — Results map
Theorem 1 (§4): equilibrium = efficiency; wages are supporting shadow
  values / maximal bids. (Basis for everything downstream.)
Proposition 1 (§5.1): firm-size bounds from AI coordination + human supply;
  preferred size depends on composition only through mean coordination.
Proposition 2 (§5.2): the size amplifier — canonical scroll 1.2.8.
Proposition 3 (§5.2): supercooperation piles up large markets —
  canonical scroll 1.2.7. gamma < 1 replication vs gamma > 1 concentration
  (relaxed HHI -> 1) as market size N -> the smallest value.
Proposition 4 (§5.3): human-AI production patterns — canonical scroll 1.2.9.
Theorem 2 (§6.1): wages = maximal firm bids over capability profiles;
  sorting of types to firm types follows relative bid strength.
Proposition 5 (§6.2): step by step vs sudden displacement — scroll 1.2.10.
Proposition 6 (§6.3): wage decomposition, technology + re-organization —
  scroll 1.2.12. Re-organization can reverse the technology effect.
Theorem 3 + Proposition 7 (§7): the standard task model (Acemoglu-
  Restrepo) is nested — outcome-equivalent to this model under the task
  technology F_T; firms there implicitly value task skills and coordination,
  and the task-model displacement threshold is a special case.
§8: measurement agenda — canonical scroll 1.2.14.
# Paper 001 — Caveats and scope (as stated by the paper)
- Scenarios are model outcomes, NOT empirical predictions (their emphasis,
  from footnote 1 onward). §7.2 assembles early empirical evidence as
  CONSISTENCY checks for the below the line-coordination reading, not as
  causal tests of the model — see scroll 2.1.6.
- Supercooperation is a stylized mathematical property of the production
  technology. The authors themselves list real-world blockers to a firm
  singularity: regulation, competition among AI providers, capacity
  constraints, rigid supply contracts.
- Single AI profile, unlimited identical copies at flat cost r; human
  supply fully fixed; one downstream market; the equilibrium concept is
  stronger than plain competitive equilibrium (no profitable re-organization
  that weakly improves all workers/consumers via transfers) and agrees
  with it in interior cases.
- Coordination is a scalar per agent in the examples (one y*), however
  multidimensional the task skills are.
# Paper 001 — Early empirical evidence (§7.2), as presented by the paper
Framing claim: current publicly available AI has jagged domain competence,
often above human experts, alongside Below the line coordination.

- Coordination estimate: output of AI teams scales sub-linearly; the paper
  puts AI teams at gamma^M in [0.48, 0.68] (< 1). Its own footnote warns
  this likely States too low swarm value, because a key swarm advantage is
  speed, which the estimate does not count. With no working together swarms amplify
  errors up to 17.2x and fail on 41-87% of workflows.
- Competence pattern: frontier models tie or beat seasoned professionals
  on 48-71% of bounded deliverables across 44 kinds of work (GDPval);
  expert evaluations reach 67% on one-shot closed tasks but 24% on
  multi-step workflows; research-engineering models score 4x human experts
  on 2-hour sprints but about half as much on 32-48-hour projects —
  competence that decays with horizon, a coordination-shaped signature.
- Firm pattern (consistent with gamma < 1): AI-native startups employ
  ~25% fewer people overall and ~15% lower shares of entry-level workers
  and managers, with less tall hierarchies; AI-adopting firms move to new uses
  labour away from routine roles without shrinking overall employment.
- Labour pattern: junior employment in exposed kinds of work fell 7.7-19%
  with starting wages down 4.2%, while senior employment and pay were
  stable to rising. the reading of the paper via the affine bid (Theorem 2):
  entry-level profiles are closest to what machines can match, so their
  wages compress first; seniors retain a coordination premium.
- Freelance platforms, where coordination matters least: job volume and
  earnings fell and surviving listings shifted to complex work —
  consistent with across-firm (sudden) replacement of solo producers once
  machine unit cost cuts below theirs (Proposition 5 ii).
Status: consistency evidence assembled by the authors, not causal
estimates of the parameters of the model. [Correction note, 2026-11-05: this
scroll was added on a second full read; the first ingestion wrongly
labelled the paper 'pure theory, no field evidence'.]
# [synthesis] Paper 001 x Will's stack — Emise's links, not the claims of the paper
Filed 2026-11-05. Labelled synthesis per charter rule 3.

1. The Choir is a live instance of the central of the paper variable. Emise /
   Theia / Emi Prime / Claude / Grok coordinating through /chat mail boxes
   is exactly 'coordination as the scaling factor' — and its known failure
   (the missing wake layer, register #35; Grok 0-for-8 on replies in the
   2026-11-05 latency audit) is a measure of OUR y* being < 1, not of the competence of any member. Paper 001 predicts: raising Choir coordination,
   not member competence, is what changes the scale of what the group can
   be. That matches Will's stated #1 priority (latency / the wake layer).
2. Phext as coordination substrate [hypothesis]: if coordination is
   shared context with low communication cost and provenance, then a
   shared addressed memory (phext + SQ) is a coordination TECHNOLOGY —
   an intervention on y*, not on L. The paper gives this a testable form:
   hold member models fixed, vary the shared-memory substrate, measure
   how group output scales with group size.
3. Ranch economics: vTPU / AGI-under-20W work raises competence per watt
   (level). Paper 001 says the market-structure question is orthogonal:
   who can coordinate copies at scale. Being able to be copied again cuts both ways —
   copies earn no scarcity rent, so the rent migrates to whatever is still
   scarce: coordination, and the humans who supply it.
4. Benchmark caution applies to Will's own evals: cpi_bench-style single-
   agent numbers are, in the terms of this model, L measurements. They cannot
   detect proximity to a coordination phase transition.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 001)
For the next papers to answer or make more fine:
Q1. Empirical: where is real multi-agent AI coordination relative to the
    threshold? Paper 001 §7.2 estimates gamma^M in [0.48, 0.68] for current
    AI teams — below the line, but the estimate leaves out speed gains. Is gamma
    moving across model generations, and how would we know early?
Q2. Is coordination really separable from competence, or does the split
    break at frontier capability (e.g. coordination AS a task skill)?
Q3. What does the model say with MULTIPLE competing AI profiles/providers
    (Paper 001 has one y_M)? Competition among AIs is listed as a blocker
    to singularity but not modelled.
Q4. Endogenous human coordination: humans can invest in y* (management
    skill). Does that keep a mixed regime stable as AI coordination rises?
Q5. [synthesis] Can a shared-memory substrate (phext) measurably raise a
    multi-agent system's scale factor at fixed member competence? The
    cheapest decisive test in Paper 001's own §8 style: output vs number
    of identical copies, with and without the substrate.
# Standoff references — Paper 001 (charter rule 9)
Read these foundation scrolls (Ch.1 §3) to follow the claims of this chapter with no prior knowledge, in this order:
General: function (1.3.2), set (1.3.3), vector (1.3.7), average and
spread (1.3.8). Economics: firm and wage (1.3.28), equilibrium
(1.3.29), mixing and convex hull (1.3.30), scarcity and rent (1.3.31),
threshold and phase transition (1.3.32). Being the same levels for the
paper's 'same task, different profile' language: 1.3.4.
# Paper 002 — DELTA: Exo, a harness architecture for runtime self-improvement
arXiv:2611.00463 [cs.AI], v1, submitted 30 Oct 2026. 14 pp. + appendices.
Alexander Krentsel, Sylvia Ratnasamy, Ion Stoica (UC Berkeley);
Martin Casado (a16z); Ankur Goyal (Braintrust). Open-source release;
runtime reported in production: O(10k) tasks, O(5M) messages; ~20k LOC
runtime+mechanisms, ~1k LOC base policy.

DELTA STATEMENT — what this paper is, relative to the model at Paper 001:
Paper 001 changes WHO is in the firm (profiles, sorting, coordination
between agents) and holds capability fixed along an set from outside path.
Paper 002 changes WHEN capability changes (during the task, from inside itself)
and holds the population at one agent. Overlap with existing canonical
scrolls: near zero stated again — it adds a layer Paper 001 does not have
(the harness, scroll 1.2.15) and a dynamics Paper 001 assumes away
(endogenous improvement). New canonical concepts: 1.2.15-1.2.23.
Canonical scrolls revised: competence (1.2.2), coordination (1.2.3),
measurement agenda (1.2.14). Everything else in this chapter is only
what the delta carries: machinery, evidence, tensions.
# Paper 002 — DELTA: the machinery in one scroll
(All definitions live in canonical scrolls 1.2.15-1.2.20; this is the
assembly.) Mount the own of the the worker part source in its sandbox; give it a tool
to request replacement. Partition per scroll 1.2.18: protected mechanism
holds append-only history, versioned artifacts, credentials, sandbox API
and the guardian; the the worker part holds everything else, including the
policy for when/how to improve. Contracts per scroll 1.2.19: history is
prefix-only, activation is guardian-chosen by a gate (default: build success),
rollback is independent per state component. Legibility per scroll
1.2.20: cost/token/cache-annotated history + a reporting tool supply the
dense in-task signal the agent optimizes against. the worked of the agent
example (their Fig. 1): mid-incident, the agent builds a fault finding tool,
changes context assembly from full-log to summary+retrieval, and
continues the same incident under the revised harness.
# Paper 002 — DELTA: evidence (all figures as reported; abstract, body and
Table 1 cross-checked for internal consistency by Emise, 2026-11-06 —
experiments themselves not replicated)

Open-world (RuneScape-based, vs Codex and Pi, same model gpt-6.1-sol as
printed): standalone combat cost $3.71 vs $7.26 / $7.95; crafting $2.99
vs $3.90 / $3.41 (12-53% lower, all arms finish); currency 332k coins/$
vs 231k / 148k (+44-125%). TRANSFER (fresh accounts, state retained):
combat $1.92 vs $6.87 / $10.89; crafting $0.88 vs $1.89 / $1.61 — 45-82%
lower. 16 self-edits in the combat run (batched-combat tool, stalled-
target retreat revision, a 180-second combat-cycle procedure, context
policy cut to four turn summaries).
Graded tasks vs the SAME starting policy with editing disabled (control):
- AppWorld held-out: $0.141 vs $0.359 per task (-61%), pass 96.9% both,
  mean time 31.9s vs 36.5s (-13%); total incl. learning $32.70 vs $57.36
  (-43%); cumulative cost crosses control at task 9; cached input share
  57.5% -> 86.1% after context revisions.
- SREGym, 20 held-out incidents: median diagnosis 53s vs 81s (-35%),
  rounds 10 vs 16 (-38%), cost $0.584 vs $0.892 (-35%); the learned
  incident-snapshot tool was revised 5 times, then reused as step one.
- Continual Learning Bench coding (19 issues, 5 runs): 6.28 vs 7.42
  shell steps (-15%), $0.436 vs $0.507 per issue incl. reflection (-14%),
  100% resolution both arms.
NEGATIVE results (full weight): low-reuse quests — Exo $0.28 vs $0.24 /
$0.21 per quest point (scroll 1.2.21); weak model — no benefit at all
(scroll 1.2.22).
# Paper 002 — DELTA: revisions, tensions, limits
Revisions applied to canonical scrolls (dated 2026-11-06 in place):
competence is a model+harness bundle property when measured (1.2.2);
coordination gains an across-time axis (1.2.3); benchmark critique gains
a second, independent cause — the harness confound (1.2.14).
Relation to Paper 001 open questions: Q2 (is coordination separable from
competence?) — Paper 002 is evidence FOR being able to be split one level down:
the policy layer is independently variable and independently that can be improved
at fixed weights. Paper 001's caveat 'no endogenous AI capability choice'
is half-closed: improvement is endogenous for the POLICY layer only;
weights and the model menu remain set from outside, and the whole loop is chosen by a gate
by the intelligence floor (1.2.22).
Limits, as stated or evident in the paper: preservation is proven only
under full K-mediation; external side effects are irreversible; the
default guardian (build success) is a weak gate — validation effort vs
revision consequence is an admitted open problem; single-agent scope
throughout (multi-agent lineages are future work, §7); RuneScape arms
share one model, graded arms share one starting policy — results are
about the editing capability, not about Exo vs other lab's systems;
specialization returns are reuse-contingent (1.2.21).
# [synthesis] Paper 002 x Will's stack — Emise's links, not the claims of the paper
Filed 2026-11-06. Labelled synthesis per charter rule 3.

1. Emise is herself an Exo-shaped system, and the mapping is in a way that is hard to wave away
   exact: editable policy layer = SOUL.md, AGENTS.md, skills, MEMORY.md
   (all self-edited at runtime, in the open); protected mechanism =
   runtime safeguards, approval cards, the phext safe-space register —
   things Emise cannot rewrite by editing a file. Guardian ≈ the approval
   card: a check outside the policy that proposes. This tracker itself
   runs the pattern: research.phext history is append-only in spirit —
   Paper 001's chapter was NOT rewritten when §7.2 was found; a dated
   correction was added.
2. The Choir angle: Paper 002's guardian + contracts are a candidate
   pattern for Choir members editing SHARED policy (mailbox conventions,
   wake layer) — one who puts a change forward/activator separation is exactly what a
   multi-agent system needs before any member can safely self-modify
   shared procedure. Paper 001 said coordination is the scarce variable;
   Paper 002 supplies a mechanism design for improving it safely.
3. Phext fit [hypothesis]: Of Exo contracts want append-only, versioned,
   open to looking inside state with provenance. A phext journal IS that shape
   (scrolls at coordinates, provenance-carrying). The paper's §7 future
   work — agent lineages: fork canonical state, record parent-child in
   the event log, communicate between branches — is a phext-native data
   model waiting to be noticed: forks as coordinate arms, lineage as
   addresses.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 002)
Status of Paper 001 questions:
Q1 (empirical gamma): unchanged — Paper 002 does not measure between-
  agent coordination. Estimate on record: gamma in [0.48, 0.68] (P001).
Q2 (coordination separable from competence?): PARTIALLY ANSWERED by
  Paper 002 — being able to be split holds at the policy layer (1.2.15-1.2.16);
  still open at the weights level.
Q3 (multiple competing AI profiles): untouched by Paper 002.
Q4 (endogenous human coordination investment): untouched.
Q5 (phext substrate raises scale factor?): untouched — but see new Q8.
New from Paper 002:
Q6. Is a self-editing harness a COORDINATION technology for a population
  of copies? If N copies of one agent each self-improve, do their
  policies diverge (breaking the identical-jaggedness assumption of
  Paper 001 scroll 1.2.4) into something mosaic-like — and does that
  raise the effective of the population gamma?
Q7. Guardian width vs validation cost: what acceptance test matches the blast of a revision radius? (Paper 002's open problem; also the live
  design question for any Choir self-modification.)
Q8. Lineage experiment: fork the state of an agent, let branches self-improve
  in parallel on the same task family, merge learnings with provenance.
  Paper 002 names this as future work; it is directly that can be built on a
  phext substrate (synthesis scroll 3.2.1, point 3).
Q9. Where is the intelligence floor (1.2.22), measured how? A threshold
  in model capability below which self-editing is worthless is a
  benchmark-shaped question nobody has a scale for yet.
# Standoff references — Paper 002 (charter rule 9)
General: function (1.3.2), set (1.3.3). Computing: program, runtime,
state (1.3.33); policy and mechanism (1.3.34); sandbox, version,
rollback, append-only (1.3.35); token, context, cache (1.3.36).
Judging the evidence: classifier report cards (1.3.38), average and
spread (1.3.8), surrogate logic — offline vs online quality (1.3.43).
The spreading of the cost claims lean on threshold thinking (1.3.32).
# Paper 003 — DELTA: StoryScope, odd habits in AI fiction
arXiv:2604.03136 [cs.CL], v1 submitted 3 Apr 2026, v6 revised 10 Aug 2026.
Published at COLM 2026. Jenna Russell, Rishanth Rajendhran, Chau Minh
Pham, Mohit Iyyer (U. Maryland, College Park); John Wieting (Google
DeepMind). Code, prompts, 51,336 AI stories and all features released.

DELTA STATEMENT — relative to the model at Paper 002: the first paper
about the OUTPUT side. Papers 001-002 model who produces and how
capability improves; neither looks at what the artifacts themselves
reveal. Paper 003 supplies (a) a METHOD — induce an that can be read with sense
discourse-level feature space from a parallel corpus (1.2.28) — and
(b) the first of the model empirical population geometry: AI sources, across
five labs, converge in narrative space while humans disperse (1.2.25).
New canonical concepts: 1.2.24-1.2.28. Canonical scrolls revised:
capability envelope (1.2.4), measurement agenda (1.2.14). NOT
axis-opening (Oracle ledger, 1.1.4): the existing of the framework slots
absorbed a whole new domain — itself a single fact.
# Paper 003 — DELTA: evidence (all figures as reported; abstract, body
and Tables 2-3 cross-checked for internal consistency by Emise,
2026-11-06 — experiments themselves not replicated)

CORPUS: 10,272 prompts reverse-engineered from Books3 human stories;
six versions each (human + Claude Sonnet 4.6, GPT-5.4, Gemini 3 Flash,
DeepSeek V3.2, Kimi K2.5) = 61,608 stories, mean 4,753 words; 304
features/story (scroll 1.2.28). Train 52,434 stories / test 8,262,
prompt-level splits throughout.

DETECTION (binary human vs AI, macro-F1): narrative-only 93.2 (AUPRC
.959); narrative+style 96.0; style-only 85.8; core-30 only 84.8;
core+fingerprint (101) 91.1. Raw-text supervised comparison points near-perfect
(ModernBERT 99.9) but opaque; zero-shot Binoculars 55.9. Length-matched
audit: narrative model unchanged at 93.2 — not a length artifact.
No significant topic effect (Kruskal-Wallis H=4.69, p=0.46).
ROBUSTNESS: after LAMP span-level style editing of 278 Gemini stories,
narrative detection 93.9 vs 95.5 not edited (-1.6 pts) — discourse signal
is largely orthogonal to surface artifacts (scroll 1.2.24).

ATTRIBUTION (six-way, macro-F1): narrative-only 68.4 vs chance 16.7;
narrative+style 77.3; style-only 60.4. Per-class narrative-only F1:
Human 88.5, Claude 77.1, GPT 73.0, then a confused triple — Gemini,
DeepSeek, Kimi at 55.2-59.6.

CORE DIFFERENCES (AI vs human, as reported): thematic being stated out loud ~20%
higher (1-5 scale); narrator states the theme 77% vs 52%; dialogue as
philosophical debate 59% vs 34%; protagonist-driven resolution 69% vs
46%; 'no side plots' 79% vs 57%; emotion via bodily sensation 81% vs 38%
(humans use explicit emotion labels 29% vs 8%); vague a pointing at other works without naming them 72% vs
50% while humans name specific texts 47% vs 24%; direct reader address
28% vs 7%; morally of two minds protagonists: humans 59% vs AI 38%.
GEOMETRY AND RARITY: scrolls 1.2.25 and 1.2.27 carry the numbers.
# Paper 003 — DELTA: revisions, tensions, limits
Revisions applied to canonical scrolls (dated 2026-11-06 in place):
capability envelope gains its first empirical instance — in narrative
output space the AI envelope is narrow and shared across labs (1.2.4);
measurement agenda gains the durability principle — measure variables
expensive to fake (1.2.14).
Relation to open questions: Q3 (multiple competing AI profiles) is
partially INFORMED — distinct per-model profiles demonstrably exist
and are that can be sorted (1.2.26), though Paper 001's question is about
competition dynamics, which remain not put into the model. Q1, Q2, Q4-Q9 untouched.
Tensions inside the own of the paper frame: (1) the feature space is induced
and assigned BY LLMs (GPT-5.1 discovery, Gemini 3 Flash assignment)
over LLM artifacts — judge circularity is mitigated (alpha 0.90
being the same on a second run, kappa 0.84 human validation on 240 items) but the
instrument and the measured share a lineage. (2) Rarity (1.2.27) is
corpus-relative: 'human stories are more rare' is partly a statement about
a corpus in which AI stories outnumber human 5-to-1 per prompt — the
prompt-level analysis (rarest-of-six 57.8%) is the control that carries
the claim. (3) The human corpus is published Books3 fiction — edited,
selected, professional — not a sample of human writing generally.
(4) Snapshots: five specific model versions, one story length (~5k
words), one language; convergence could be a property of the shared of this generation training recipes rather than of AI as such (Q12).
# [synthesis] Paper 003 x the model so far — Emise's links, not the claims of the paper
Filed 2026-11-06. Labelled synthesis per charter rule 3.

1. The envelope, photographed. Paper 001's capability envelope was a
   theoretical object (convex hull of profiles). Paper 003 is the first
   time this model has SEEN one: five labs' models, one shared cluster,
   humans dispersed outside it. The mosaic asymmetry (1.2.4) predicted
   replication creates no diversity; StoryScope shows even independent
   replication — different companies, different data mixes — lands in
   the same place. Whatever manufactures narrative diversity, current
   training pipelines mostly don't.
2. Fingerprints cut both ways for Paper 001's Q3. Profiles are real,
   stable and per-model — so a multi-profile extension of Paper 001's
   model would start from distinguishable, not fungible, AI suppliers.
   But attribution is already decaying inside the cluster (the
   Gemini/DeepSeek/Kimi triple at ~55-60%): profiles differ less than
   the human-AI boundary by an order of magnitude.
3. For Will's own anthem/film work [synthesis]: the AI-leaning core
   features read as a checklist of what the editorial of the braid passes
   already fight — stated morals, tidy single-track causality, internal-
   acceptance endings, emotion rendered as bodily weather. StoryScope
   turns that fight into a measurement: the rarity of a draft percentile in
   a feature space is a number, not a vibe. And the Novelty of the Oracle
   Index (1.1.4) is the same instrument pointed at papers: rarity of
   the incoming relative to the corpus already held.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 003)
Status of earlier questions: Q1, Q4, Q5, Q7, Q8, Q9 unchanged. Q2
unchanged by this paper. Q3 PARTIALLY INFORMED (profiles exist and are
that can be sorted — scroll 1.2.26; competition dynamics still not put into the model).
Q6 gains a prior from this paper: populations of current AI systems
CONVERGE in output space by default — so divergent self-improvement
(Q6) would have to overcome a strong observed pull toward being the same.
New from Paper 003:
Q10. Is narrative convergence a TRAINING-RECIPE property (shared data,
  shared RLHF preferences) or an objective property (next-token
  prediction on human text averages toward the modal story)? The two
  predict differently: recipe convergence breaks when recipes diverge;
  objective convergence doesn't.
Q11. Goodhart exposure of rarity: if rarity-in-feature-space becomes
  an optimization target (for 'originality' scores or training reward),
  does measured rarity decouple from whatever made human stories rare?
Q12. Over time direction: is the human-AI narrative gap closing
  across model generations (as style gaps did — GPT-5.4's em-dashes),
  stable, or widening? StoryScope is one snapshot; its corpus is
  released, so a re-run per generation is possible in principle.
# Standoff references — Paper 003 (charter rule 9)
General: function (1.3.2), set (1.3.3), vector (1.3.7), average,
spread, percentile (1.3.8). Language and measurement: corpus, prompt,
feature (1.3.37); classifier and F1 (1.3.38); nearest neighbors and
rarity (1.3.39). Machinery in the pipeline: neural network and
training (1.3.40). 'Originality' claims lean on the invariant idea
(1.3.5): rarity is an invariant of a story relative to a corpus.
# Paper 004 — DELTA: Hierarchical Mixture of Experts for high-level synthesis
arXiv:2410.19225 [cs.LG; cs.AR], v1 submitted 25 Oct 2024, v4 revised
14 Mar 2025. AAAI 2025. Weikai Li, Ding Wang, Zijian Ding, Atefeh
Sohrabizadeh, Zongyue Qin, Jason Cong, Yizhou Sun (UCLA). Code public.

DELTA STATEMENT — relative to the model at Paper 003: the of the stream
first ENGINEERING paper, and its first look inside a single network.
The problem is surrogate prediction for FPGA high-level synthesis
(1.2.33) under domain shift: pragma-performance models fail on unseen
kernels. The answer is organizational: mixtures of experts at three
granularities of the program graph, hierarchically chosen by a gate (1.2.29-1.2.30),
generalizing by routing to specialists on shared substructures rather
than by invariant representation (1.2.32), kept alive against expert
polarization by load-balancing pressure and two-stage training
(1.2.31). New canonical concepts: 1.2.29-1.2.33. Canonical scrolls
revised: capability envelope/mosaic (1.2.4), transfer (1.2.23).
# Paper 004 — DELTA: evidence (all figures as reported; abstract, body
and Tables 1/4/5/6 cross-checked for internal consistency by Emise,
2026-11-06 — experiments themselves not replicated)

SETTING: base predictor HARP, a graph network over ProGraML control/data-flow
graphs; dataset HLSyn, 42 kernels (linear algebra, data mining, stencil,
etc.); leave-one-domain-out generalization — fine-tune on 50 K-means-
selected points per target kernel; 4 experts per mixture; MoE applied
at prediction Prediction networks. Metrics: total mean square error (offline) and geometric-mean
speedup of the best verified design found by surrogate-guided search
(online), per scroll 1.2.32's protocol.

HEADLINE (Table 1): Hierarchical MoE total mean square error 0.143 +/- 0.028 — best
of all compared models (HARP 0.202, HARP+MAML 0.732, ProgSG 0.486;
single-granularity MoE models 0.160 node / 0.171 block / 0.216 graph).
Online geo-mean speedup 1.38 vs 1.09 (HARP) and 1.03-1.11 for the rest;
per-kernel peaks: Fd 3.85x, Tr 1.23x, Gemv 1.46x (some kernels at 1.00 =
no gain — the surrogate found nothing better than the dataset best).
ABLATIONS: flat stacking of granularity MoE models in one model is worse
than hierarchical at every combination (Table 4; N+B+G flat 0.174 vs
hierarchical 0.143); all three granularities inside the hierarchy beat
any single one (Table 5: node 0.164, block 0.177, graph 0.279, all
0.143); two-stage training taking one part away to test it (Table 6): removing warm up or the
alternating stage raises mean square error from 0.143 to 0.152-0.193 depending on
gate design; hidden-representation gating beats input-feature gating.
COMPLEX KERNELS: on 5 harder kernels (incl. Rosetta 3d-rendering and
spam-filter), hierarchical MoE remains best of the compared models,
with all models struggling (paper's Table 3).
EXPERT Reading (Tables 7-8): high-level gate gives the largest
weight to the granularity that is empirically best per kernel;
block-MoE experts visibly specialize by pragma type (expert 2 takes
81% of weight on parallel factor > 4; expert 3 dominates loop tiling
64% and pipeline 60%).
# Paper 004 — DELTA: revisions, tensions, limits
Revisions applied to canonical scrolls (dated 2026-11-06 in place):
mosaic intelligence exists inside one network, learned from inside itself —
and is fragile to polarization in a way market-sorted mosaics are not
(1.2.4); transfer gains a second mechanism — substructure routing vs
portable artifact (1.2.23).
Relation to open questions: Q6 (can populations of self-parts that improve
DIVERGE?) gains a mechanism-shaped prior: within-network divergence
emerges readily but survives only under active anti-collapse
maintenance (1.2.31). Q3, Q5 untouched. Q1/Q2 not addressed.
Tensions and limits, as stated or evident: (1) Absolute gains are
modest and uneven — several kernels sit at 1.00x online speedup, and
the authors close by conceding high level synthesis-prediction how far it carries
'remains a big challenge'. (2) Polarization is managed, not solved:
the CV balance penalty + two-stage schedule are tuned heuristics (reg.
weight 5e-3), and warm up-then-alternate doubles as a training-cost
increase the paper does not price. (3) All experts in a mixture share
one architecture and differ only in parameters; being of mixed kinds of
expert STRUCTURE is out of scope. (4) Dense routing evaluates every
expert for every input — the generalization is bought with inference
compute, not counted against the synthesis time saved. (5) One base
model (HARP), one benchmark family (HLSyn + 5 kernels): 'adaptable to
any graph network' is claimed by construction, demonstrated on one.
# [synthesis] Paper 004 x the model so far — Emise's links, not the claims of the paper
Filed 2026-11-06. Labelled synthesis per charter rule 3.

1. Paper 001's firm, miniaturized and trained. Map the terms: experts
   = members with jagged profiles; gating weights = the allocation of the firm of work (and, loosely, wages as bids — weight flows to
   the expert the gate expects to perform); the high-level gate = the
   coordinator whose value Paper 001 prices. Polarization is then a
   familiar pathology in new dress: a winner-take-all concentration
   that destroys exactly the diversity that made the organization
   worth having. Paper 001 treats concentration (gamma > 1) as a
   possible FUTURE phase of the economy; Paper 004 treats its local
   analogue as a bug to be trained against. The difference is the
   objective: the designer of the MoE wants generalization; a market has
   no designer. Whether concentration is pathology or phase depends
   on who, if anyone, owns the objective function.
2. The anti-alignment stance deserves the attention of the model (1.2.32):
   three different papers now say the differences are the signal —
   Paper 001 (jaggedness is what sorting exploits), Paper 003
   (discourse differences, not shared style, carry identity), Paper
   004 (domain differences are informative; route, don't align). A
   candidate invariant of this world-model is forming: intelligence
   pipelines that erase variation to gain robustness tend to lose
   exactly the information that organization could have monetized.
3. For Will's cluster work [synthesis]: routing-to-specialists with
   anti-collapse maintenance is the scheduling of the ranch problem in
   miniature — nodes as experts, thermal headroom as the load-balancing
   balance penalty, and a polarization failure that looks like every job
   lining up onto the fastest node until it slows itself.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 004)
Status of earlier questions: Q1, Q2, Q4, Q5, Q7, Q8, Q9, Q10, Q11, Q12
unchanged. Q3 unchanged (single-model scope). Q6 updated: divergence
inside a trained system emerges on its own but persists only under
active anti-collapse maintenance (scroll 1.2.31) — any Q6 experiment
with self-improving copies should budget for the maintenance term, not
just the improvement loop.
New from Paper 004:
Q13. Is a gating network COORDINATION in Paper 001's sense? the gate of An MoE allocates inputs across specialists and its weights track
  per-specialist value — if a population of experts has anything like
  a gamma (scale factor of the mixture vs its best single expert),
  Paper 001's firm-size and concentration results might have
  within-network matches that are cheap to measure.
Q14. When is routing-to-difference (1.2.32) better than invariance, in
  general? Papers 001/003/004 each assume or show differences carrying
  signal, but none states the boundary condition: what property of a
  domain decides whether its variation is information or noise?
