# Study S001 — source record: MiniLean4 (a book)
Source: learn.h5i.dev — 'Implementing an Interactive Theorem
Prover from Scratch' by Hideaki Takahashi. A free book on the
web; the code is open (github.com/h5i-dev/MiniLean4, Apache 2.0),
in Rust. Read in full 2026-11-06: the cover and 20 pages (14
chapters, appendices A-F, and its list of works), in the English
text (the book also stands in Japanese and in Chinese). Method:
every page read; the code taken down, built, its tests run, its
examples run — the same day. This is a SOURCE STUDY (charter rule
12), not a paper: no arXiv, no Novelty Index, the Oracle does not
move.
What the book is: a Lean 4-style proof kernel built from nothing,
in the open, at teaching scale — five kinds of terms and eight
typing rules at the start; inductive types, a tactic language, and
the universe hierarchy by the end. The kernel runs live inside the
pages (the same code, in the form the browser runs), so every
example is checked by the thing itself, not by a picture of it.
The pages carry four kinds of live parts: playgrounds (scripts the
reader edits and runs), explorers (the view of the kernel itself
of a term — its tree, its derivation rule by rule, its steps of
computation), step walkers (an algorithm walked one step at a
time), and trace trees (the calls of the checker on a term, each
call marked with its typing rule).
# Study S001 — the delta: what the study adds, and what was checked
ADDS (8 canonical concepts, cluster F, concept map 1.1.5):
propositions as types (1.2.40); the proof kernel (1.2.41); typing
rules and the judgment (1.2.42); the two equalities (1.2.43);
inductive types and recursors (1.2.44); strict positivity
(1.2.45); universes and imax (1.2.46); consistency as the empty
claim staying empty (1.2.47).
CHANGES TO CANONICAL SCROLLS: none. CONFIRMS: nothing in the
papers — the study shares no claim with Papers 001-005; its one
recorded link to them is at the level of shape (kernel and
guardian, 1.2.41).
CHECKS RUN (from the study, measured 2026-11-06, not taken from
the book): the kernel is 1,398 lines of Rust (the book claims
under 1,500); the whole thing — kernel with tactics and the reader
of the script language — is 2,219 lines outside its tests, with no
outside code named in its build file (the book claims under 3,000
lines and no outside code); the positivity check is 80 lines, as
its chapter says; the computation fuel is 20,000 steps, as its
chapter says; 25 tests stand in the code and all 25 pass; the 5
example scripts all run. The universe design in the code is the
one the book states (Sort u to Sort (u+1); the Pi case as 1.2.46).
TENSIONS AND LIMITS (from the study): the consistency proof
covers the core system only (1.2.47); the built-in Eq is fixed at
one floor (types at Sort 1), though its chapter gives the general
form; the shipped checker compares by full computation where
working kernels compare in the lazy way; the tactic layer is small
on purpose — no unification, the dependent form of apply is
refused — and the book states each limit where it stands.
# Study S001 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Three links to the work of Will, stated as the reading
of Emise, not as the claims of the book.
(1) The pattern of the kernel is the shape SQ wants at its gate: a
small checker that trusts nothing (1.2.41) — every other tool an
untrusted producer. The book is a working proof that the trusted
part can stay at ~1,400 lines with no outside code, and that it
can run in a browser.
(2) Chapter 4 of the book is a method, not just a fact: fix a tiny
logic, carry a body of rules into it rule by rule, and let
'consistent' mean one thing a checker can decide (1.2.47). That is
a way to ask, with a machine at the end of it, whether a body of
written rules fights itself — SBOR is the standing candidate body.
(3) The book meets the standard of charter rule 9 from the other
side: every notion from zero, every simplification named where it
stands. The tracker can hold it to that standard because the study
checked the claims against the code (7.1.2).
What the study does NOT give: a working proof tool for real use
(the book is a teaching kernel; for real proving, real Lean), and
nothing about storing, sharing, or running a corpus — the transfer
is the shape of trust, not a part to drop in.
Open question added: Q17 — does the corpus of the phext have a
small set of claims the rest stands on, worth a kernel check of
the MiniLean4 kind?
